window.REVISION_SUBJECTS.push({"config":{"brandIcon":"⚖️","brandSubtitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) · Component 2","brandTitle":"Themes A–F (AQA RS A)","breadcrumb":"Religious Studies / AQA A / Themes","examBoard":"AQA GCSE","footerNote":"Original practice questions written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification, using the question pattern for exams from June 2026 (1, 1, 4, 6 and 12 marks per theme, plus 3 SPaG marks on the 12-mark question). Not past-paper material. Model answers are indicative exemplars, not official AQA mark schemes; other valid answers are credited.","heroEyebrow":"Component 2 · Themes A–F · answer four themes","pageTitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A – Thematic Studies Revision","papers":[{"id":"Theme A","label":"Theme A: Relationships and families","subtitle":"sex, marriage and divorce · families and gender equality","title":"Relationships and families"},{"id":"Theme B","label":"Theme B: Religion and life","subtitle":"origins and value of the universe · origins and value of human life","title":"Religion and life"},{"id":"Theme C","label":"Theme C: The existence of God and revelation","subtitle":"arguments for and against God · the divine and revelation","title":"The existence of God and revelation"},{"id":"Theme D","label":"Theme D: Religion, peace and conflict","subtitle":"violence, terrorism and war · 21st-century conflict","title":"Religion, peace and conflict"},{"id":"Theme E","label":"Theme E: Religion, crime and punishment","subtitle":"crime and its causes · religion and punishment","title":"Religion, crime and punishment"},{"id":"Theme F","label":"Theme F: Religion, human rights and social justice","subtitle":"human rights · wealth and poverty","title":"Religion, human rights and social justice"}],"storageId":"aqa_rs_a_themes","subjectArea":"Religious Studies","subjectName":"Thematic studies"},"questions":{"paper1":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-1-0","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.1","moduleTitle":"Human sexuality and sexual relationships before and outside marriage","question":"Which one of the following is the term for a married person having a sexual relationship with someone who is not their spouse?\nA Cohabitation\nB Chastity\nC Adultery\nD Celibacy","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C Adultery.\nAdultery is sex between a married person and someone other than their husband or wife. Cohabitation is living together without marriage, chastity is not having sex before marriage, and celibacy is choosing not to have sex at all.","evidence":["Exodus 20:14 \"You shall not commit adultery\""],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"adultery"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-1-1","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.1","moduleTitle":"Human sexuality and sexual relationships before and outside marriage","question":"Give one religious reason why some believers think sex should only take place within marriage.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: sex is a gift from God for a husband and wife to become 'one flesh'; sex is meant to be open to having children, who should be raised in a stable married family; scripture forbids sexual immorality/adultery; it shows respect for the body as a temple of the Holy Spirit.","evidence":["Genesis 2:24","1 Corinthians 6:18–19"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"sex before marriage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-1-2","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.1","moduleTitle":"Human sexuality and sexual relationships before and outside marriage","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about sexual relationships before marriage.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Muslims believe that sex before marriage is always wrong. The Qur'an forbids zina and tells believers not even to 'go near' it (17:32), so Muslims are expected to remain chaste, and families often discourage dating so that sex is kept for the commitment of nikah.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, some liberal Christians in Britain accept sex before marriage for a couple in a loving, committed relationship. They argue that the deeper Christian principle is love and faithfulness rather than the legal ceremony, so a couple who are committed and intend to marry are not acting selfishly or exploitatively.","evidence":["Qur'an 17:32","Liberal Christian emphasis on love and commitment (agape)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"sex before marriage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-1-3","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.1","moduleTitle":"Human sexuality and sexual relationships before and outside marriage","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about homosexual relationships.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Catholic Church teaches that homosexual people must be treated with respect and never unjustly discriminated against, but that homosexual sexual acts are wrong. This is because they go against natural law: sex is intended to be both unitive and open to procreation within marriage between a man and a woman.\n\nBelief 2: Reform and Liberal Jews in Britain accept homosexual relationships and their rabbis can conduct same-sex weddings. They believe every person is created in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), so a loving, faithful same-sex relationship has the same value as a heterosexual one, and they read prohibitions such as Leviticus 18:22 in their historical context.","evidence":["Catechism of the Catholic Church 2357–2359","Genesis 1:27","Leviticus 18:22","Reform/Liberal Judaism same-sex marriages"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"homosexuality"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-1-4","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.1","moduleTitle":"Human sexuality and sexual relationships before and outside marriage","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about adultery.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe adultery is a serious sin because it breaks the promises of faithfulness made before God in the marriage vows. The Ten Commandments state 'You shall not commit adultery' (Exodus 20:14), and Jesus went further, teaching that anyone who looks at someone lustfully has already committed adultery in their heart (Matthew 5:27–28). So Christians are taught to guard their thoughts as well as their actions.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe adultery is a major sin that damages families and society. The Qur'an says 'Do not go near adultery; it is an outrage and an evil path' (17:32), which means Muslims should avoid situations that could lead to it, such as being alone with someone of the opposite sex who is not a close relative. Adultery betrays a spouse, can bring children into an unstable situation, and harms trust in the community.","evidence":["Exodus 20:14","Matthew 5:27–28","Qur'an 17:32"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"adultery"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-1-5","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.1","moduleTitle":"Human sexuality and sexual relationships before and outside marriage","question":"'Sex should only take place within marriage.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany Christians agree with the statement because they believe sex is a gift from God designed for marriage. Genesis 2:24 describes a man and woman becoming 'one flesh' after leaving their parents and being united, which suggests the physical union belongs inside the lifelong commitment of marriage. The Catholic Church teaches that sex has two purposes, unitive and procreative, and both are best fulfilled within marriage, where any child is born into a stable home with two committed parents. Therefore sex outside marriage is seen as separating sex from the commitment it is meant to express.\n\nMuslims and Orthodox Jews would also agree. The Qur'an forbids zina and tells believers not to 'go near' adultery (17:32). Keeping sex for marriage is seen as protecting individuals, particularly women and children, from being used or abandoned, and protecting the family as the foundation of society. A further argument is practical: marriage brings legal protection and public promises, so if a pregnancy occurs the child has security.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOther people disagree. Humanists argue that consenting adults should be free to make their own choices about sex, and that what matters morally is consent, honesty and avoiding harm rather than whether a couple has had a wedding. Many couples in Britain cohabit in long-term, faithful relationships, raise children and are just as committed as married couples. Contraception also means sex need not result in pregnancy, which weakens the argument based on protecting children.\n\nSome liberal Christians argue that the central Christian teaching is love (agape) and faithfulness. Jesus did not specifically discuss sex before marriage, and they argue that a committed couple who intend to marry are acting in the spirit of Christian love. Buddhists judge sexual behaviour by the third Precept – avoiding sexual misconduct – which focuses on whether a relationship causes harm, rather than on marital status.\n\nCONCLUSION\nBoth sides are concerned about the same thing: protecting people from being hurt and treating sex as something meaningful. The argument for the statement is strongest for those who accept religious authority, because scripture in several religions clearly links sex and marriage. However, in a diverse society not everyone accepts those authorities, and the evidence that loving, committed unmarried couples can provide stability weakens the claim that sex must 'only' take place within marriage. I conclude that for religious believers the statement is justified by their sources of authority, but as a universal rule it is too absolute; commitment, consent and responsibility are the essential moral conditions.","evidence":["Genesis 2:24","Qur'an 17:32","Catholic teaching: unitive and procreative purposes of sex","Third Buddhist Moral Precept","Humanist emphasis on consent and harm"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"sex before marriage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-2-0","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.2","moduleTitle":"Contraception and family planning","question":"Which one of the following is a method of family planning accepted by the Catholic Church?\nA The contraceptive pill\nB Natural family planning\nC Condoms\nD Sterilisation","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Natural family planning.\nHumanae Vitae teaches that each sexual act must stay open to life, so artificial methods (A, C, D) are rejected, but couples may use the woman's natural fertility cycle to space children for serious reasons.","evidence":["Humanae Vitae (1968)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"contraception"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-2-1","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.2","moduleTitle":"Contraception and family planning","question":"Give one reason why a religious couple might decide to use contraception.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: to protect the mother's health; to space out their children; because they cannot afford to care for more children; to avoid passing on a genetic disorder; to prevent the spread of a sexually transmitted infection.","evidence":["Most Muslims and Protestants accept family planning for health or financial reasons"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"contraception"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-2-2","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.2","moduleTitle":"Contraception and family planning","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about the use of artificial contraception.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Catholic Church teaches that artificial contraception is wrong. In Humanae Vitae (1968) it states that every sexual act within marriage must be open to new life, so deliberately blocking conception goes against natural law and God's purpose for sex; Catholics who need to space children should use natural family planning instead.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, most Muslims in Britain accept artificial contraception within marriage for good reasons such as the mother's health or spacing children. This is because hadith record that 'azl (withdrawal) was practised by Companions of the Prophet without being forbidden, so preventing conception is not seen as wrong, provided the couple do not use it to avoid having children altogether.","evidence":["Humanae Vitae (1968)","Hadith on 'azl (Sahih Muslim)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"contraception"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-2-3","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.2","moduleTitle":"Contraception and family planning","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about the use of contraception within marriage.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Church of England accepts contraception within marriage. Since the Lambeth Conference of 1930 Anglicans have taught that couples may use other methods of birth control 'in the light of Christian principles', so planning a family responsibly, for example to give each child proper care, is seen as a loving decision.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, many Sikhs in Britain believe contraception is acceptable and is a decision for the married couple. The Guru Granth Sahib does not forbid it, and Sikhs value equality between husband and wife, so they believe the couple should agree together about how many children they can raise well, though methods that end a life once conceived would be rejected.","evidence":["Lambeth Conference 1930","Sikh teaching on equality of husband and wife"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"contraception"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-2-4","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.2","moduleTitle":"Contraception and family planning","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about why couples should be open to having children.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe that having children is one of the main purposes of marriage and a way of sharing in God's creation. In Genesis 1:28 God blesses the first humans and commands them to 'be fruitful and increase in number', so children are seen as a gift and blessing from God, not a burden. The Catholic Church therefore teaches that married couples should be generous in welcoming new life.\n\nBelief 2: Jews believe that 'be fruitful and multiply' is the first mitzvah (commandment) in the Torah. Raising children continues the Jewish people and passes on the covenant with God from generation to generation, for example by teaching children the Shema and celebrating Shabbat together. Orthodox Jews therefore normally use contraception only for serious reasons such as a threat to the mother's health.","evidence":["Genesis 1:28","Mitzvah of 'be fruitful and multiply'","Deuteronomy 6:7 (teach them to your children)"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"procreation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-2-5","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.2","moduleTitle":"Contraception and family planning","question":"'Religious believers should be free to use any form of contraception.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany people would agree. Most Protestant Christians, including the Church of England since the Lambeth Conference of 1930, believe that responsible family planning is a moral choice for a married couple. They argue that God gave humans reason and freedom, and using contraception so that each child can be properly cared for, or to protect a woman's health, is a loving and responsible use of that freedom. Many Sikhs and Muslims also accept contraception for good reasons. Humanists add that contraception reduces unwanted pregnancies and abortions, prevents the spread of STIs and HIV, and gives women control over their own bodies and lives, all of which increase human wellbeing.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nHowever, the word 'any' makes the statement harder to accept. The Catholic Church teaches in Humanae Vitae that every sexual act within marriage must remain open to life, so artificial methods are wrong because they separate the unitive and procreative purposes of sex. Catholics may only use natural family planning. Even religions that generally accept contraception draw limits. Many Christians, Muslims, Hindus and Buddhists reject methods that might work after conception, such as some forms of emergency contraception, because they believe life begins at conception and the principle of sanctity of life (or ahimsa, non-harming) forbids ending it. Most Muslims also say contraception should not be used to avoid having children altogether, because children are a blessing and the family is central in Islam. Orthodox Jews often prefer some methods over others because of the teaching against 'wasting seed'.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThere is a strong case that believers should be free to plan their families, because almost every religion accepts at least some form of family planning, and it can protect health and wellbeing. However, the claim that they should be free to use 'any' form is weaker, because many religious traditions draw a clear moral line at methods that destroy a life after conception, and Catholic teaching rejects all artificial methods. I conclude that the statement is only partly true: in a free society believers have a legal right to choose, but for many of them their faith rightly guides which methods are morally acceptable, so in practice their choice is shaped by conscience and teaching rather than being unlimited.","evidence":["Humanae Vitae (1968)","Lambeth Conference 1930","Sanctity of life / ahimsa","Qur'an 17:31","Humanist arguments on wellbeing and autonomy"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"contraception"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-3-0","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.3","moduleTitle":"Marriage, same-sex marriage, cohabitation, divorce and remarriage","question":"Which one of the following is the term for a declaration that a marriage was never valid?\nA Annulment\nB Divorce\nC Cohabitation\nD Remarriage","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A Annulment.\nAn annulment (granted, for example, by a Catholic marriage tribunal) states that a valid marriage never existed, e.g. because one partner did not freely consent. Divorce ends a marriage that did exist.","evidence":["Catholic teaching on annulment"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"divorce"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-3-1","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.3","moduleTitle":"Marriage, same-sex marriage, cohabitation, divorce and remarriage","question":"Give one purpose of marriage in religious teaching.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: lifelong companionship/love and support; to have children; to provide a stable home to raise children in the faith; to make a commitment before God; a proper context for sex.","evidence":["Church of England marriage service","Genesis 2:24"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"marriage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-3-2","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.3","moduleTitle":"Marriage, same-sex marriage, cohabitation, divorce and remarriage","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about divorce.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Catholic Church teaches that a valid sacramental marriage cannot be dissolved by divorce. This is because the couple make vows before God for life, and Jesus taught 'what God has joined together, let no one separate' (Mark 10:9), so Catholics can separate legally but can only remarry in church if their first marriage is annulled.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, Muslims believe divorce is permitted, although it is disliked. If attempts at reconciliation through family arbiters fail, a couple may divorce (talaq) after a waiting period of three months (iddah), and both partners are free to remarry, because Islam recognises that forcing people to stay in a broken marriage can cause greater harm.","evidence":["Mark 10:9","Qur'an 4:35","Iddah waiting period"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"divorce"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-3-3","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.3","moduleTitle":"Marriage, same-sex marriage, cohabitation, divorce and remarriage","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about same-sex marriage.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Church of England teaches that marriage in church is between one man and one woman, so it does not conduct same-sex weddings. However, since 2023 it has permitted prayers of blessing (Prayers of Love and Faith) for same-sex couples, showing that it wants to welcome them while keeping its traditional definition of marriage.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, Reform and Liberal Judaism in Britain conduct same-sex marriages. They believe that all people are made in the image of God and that a loving, lifelong commitment between two people of the same sex should be celebrated in the synagogue in the same way as any other marriage.","evidence":["Church of England Prayers of Love and Faith (2023)","Genesis 1:27","Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"same-sex marriage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-3-4","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.3","moduleTitle":"Marriage, same-sex marriage, cohabitation, divorce and remarriage","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about the nature and purpose of marriage.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe that marriage is a lifelong covenant between a man and a woman made before God. Genesis 2:24 says that 'a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and they become one flesh', which shows that marriage creates a new, permanent union. This is why the vows promise faithfulness 'till death us do part', and why many Christians see divorce as breaking a sacred promise.\n\nBelief 2: Sikhs believe that marriage is a spiritual union that helps both partners grow closer to God (Waheguru). In the Anand Karaj ceremony the couple walk around the Guru Granth Sahib four times while the Lavan hymns are sung, and the Guru Granth Sahib teaches that they are not those who merely sit together, but 'one light in two bodies'. So the purpose of marriage is equal partnership and shared spiritual progress, as well as raising a family.","evidence":["Genesis 2:24","Guru Granth Sahib, Lavan (Guru Ram Das)","'One light in two bodies' (Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 788)"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"marriage"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-3-5","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.3","moduleTitle":"Marriage, same-sex marriage, cohabitation, divorce and remarriage","question":"'Religious people should never get divorced.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nSome religious believers agree because of the sanctity of marriage vows. In a Christian wedding the couple promise before God to stay together 'for better, for worse… till death us do part'. Jesus taught 'what God has joined together, let no one separate' (Mark 10:9), and the Catholic Church teaches that a valid sacramental marriage cannot be dissolved; it may only be declared null by annulment. Breaking such a promise is seen as breaking a promise to God, not only to a partner. Supporters also argue that divorce can harm children, who need stability, and that if divorce is easy, people may give up on marriage too quickly instead of working through difficulties with forgiveness, which is central to many faiths. Hindus traditionally see marriage as a lifelong sacramental bond for the same reason.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOthers argue that 'never' is too strong. The argument from compassion says that forcing someone to stay in an abusive, violent or loveless marriage causes great suffering, and a loving God would not want that. Jesus himself allowed an exception for sexual immorality (Matthew 19:9), and the Church of England has allowed remarriage in church, at the priest's discretion, since 2002, recognising that relationships sometimes fail and people deserve a second chance. Islam permits divorce, even though a hadith says it is 'the most hated of lawful things to Allah', because it is better than continued harm; the Qur'an requires family arbiters to attempt reconciliation first (4:35). Judaism also allows divorce through a get. Humanists argue that when a relationship has irretrievably broken down, ending it kindly is the most honest and least harmful choice, and UK law has accepted this with no-fault divorce since 2022.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe arguments for the statement show how seriously religions take marriage, and it is right that religious people should work hard to keep their vows and seek reconciliation before considering divorce. However, I find the compassion argument more convincing, because a rule of 'never' would trap victims of abuse and does not reflect the teaching of most religions, which permit divorce as a last resort. Therefore I disagree with the statement: divorce should be rare and not entered lightly, but it can be the right choice for a religious person when a marriage has truly broken down.","evidence":["Mark 10:9","Matthew 19:9","Qur'an 4:35","Hadith: divorce the most hated lawful thing","Church of England remarriage (2002)","No-fault divorce (2022)"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"divorce"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-4-0","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.4","moduleTitle":"Families and gender equality","question":"Which one of the following is the term for a family made up of parents, children and other relatives such as grandparents?\nA Nuclear family\nB Extended family\nC Blended family\nD Single-parent family","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Extended family.\nAn extended family includes relatives beyond the parents and children, such as grandparents, aunts and uncles. A nuclear family is just parents and children.","evidence":["Definition: extended family"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"family types"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-4-1","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.4","moduleTitle":"Families and gender equality","question":"Give one example of gender discrimination.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: paying a woman less than a man for the same job; refusing to promote someone because of their sex; refusing a job to a woman because she might become pregnant; denying girls education; stopping someone holding a religious role only because of their sex (accept as an example of a debated issue).","evidence":["Equality Act 2010"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"gender discrimination"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-4-2","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.4","moduleTitle":"Families and gender equality","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about same-sex parents.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Catholic Church teaches that children have a right to be brought up by a mother and a father. It believes that God's design for the family is based on the marriage of a man and a woman, and that both male and female role models help a child to develop, so it does not support adoption by same-sex couples.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many liberal Christians and Reform Jews in Britain support same-sex parents. They believe that what matters most for a child is a loving, stable and safe home, and that same-sex couples who adopt or foster are showing the kind of selfless love God calls for; UK law has allowed same-sex couples to adopt since 2005.","evidence":["Catechism of the Catholic Church on the family","Adoption and Children Act 2002 (in force 2005)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"same-sex parents"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-4-3","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.4","moduleTitle":"Families and gender equality","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about gender equality.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe that men and women are equal because both were created in the image of God (Genesis 1:27). This is why the Church of England has ordained women as priests since 1994 and as bishops since 2014, believing that women can serve God in leadership as fully as men.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, Sikhs believe men and women are completely equal. Guru Nanak criticised those who looked down on women, asking why anyone would call woman bad 'from whom kings are born', so in the gurdwara women can read the Guru Granth Sahib, lead prayers and serve in the langar in the same way as men.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 473","Church of England women bishops 2014"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"gender equality"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-4-4","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.4","moduleTitle":"Families and gender equality","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about the role of children in a family.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe that children should respect and obey their parents. The fifth commandment says 'Honour your father and your mother' (Exodus 20:12), and Paul repeats this in Ephesians 6:1, so children are expected to listen to their parents' guidance and, as adults, to care for their parents when they grow old.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe that children have a duty of kindness and care towards their parents, especially in old age. The Qur'an teaches 'your Lord has commanded that you worship none but Him, and be kind to parents… do not say to them a word of disrespect' (17:23). This means Muslim children often look after elderly parents at home rather than putting them in a care home, and a hadith teaches that 'Paradise lies at the feet of your mother'.","evidence":["Exodus 20:12","Ephesians 6:1–4","Qur'an 17:23–24","Hadith: Paradise at the feet of mothers (an-Nasa'i)"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"role of children"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-4-5","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.4","moduleTitle":"Families and gender equality","question":"'Men and women should have the same roles in family life.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany Christians support the statement because Genesis 1:27 teaches that God created humanity 'male and female' and both are made in God's image, so neither is superior. Paul wrote that in Christ 'there is neither… male nor female' (Galatians 3:28). Many modern Christian families share childcare, housework and paid work equally, believing that love means serving one another. Sikhs also argue for equality: the Gurus taught that women should be respected and that husband and wife are equal partners, 'one light in two bodies'. Humanists and supporters of the Equality Act 2010 argue that fixing roles by gender limits people's freedom and talents, and that children benefit from seeing both parents care and work. In modern Britain, many families rely on two incomes, so practical needs also support shared roles.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOthers believe men and women are equal in value but have different, complementary roles. Some Christians read Ephesians 5:22–25 as giving the husband a role of loving leadership and the wife a role of support, though they stress that the husband must love his wife 'as Christ loved the church'. Many Muslims believe that men have the responsibility to provide and protect (Qur'an 4:34), while women have a special honour as mothers, reflected in the hadith 'Paradise lies at the feet of your mother'. Orthodox Jews see the mother as the heart of the Jewish home, teaching children and preparing for Shabbat, while men have more public religious duties such as praying in a minyan. Supporters argue that different roles are not inferior roles and that equality does not have to mean sameness.\n\nCONCLUSION\nBoth views agree that men and women are of equal worth, which is the most important point. The complementary view is sincerely held and, when freely chosen, can work well. However, I find the statement more convincing, because insisting on roles based only on gender can easily turn into discrimination and can stop individuals using their gifts; it also ignores the reality of single-parent and same-sex families. My conclusion is that roles should be shared or decided by each couple fairly and freely, rather than fixed by gender, which fits best with the shared religious belief that every person is made in the image of God.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Galatians 3:28","Ephesians 5:22–25","Qur'an 4:34","Equality Act 2010"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"roles of men and women"}],"paper2":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-1-0","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.1","moduleTitle":"The origins of the universe","question":"Which one of the following means 'out of nothing'?\nA Ex nihilo\nB Big Bang\nC Stewardship\nD Evolution","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A Ex nihilo.\nChristians, Muslims and Jews believe God created the universe ex nihilo – out of nothing – rather than from pre-existing material.","evidence":["Genesis 1:1"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"creation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-1-1","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.1","moduleTitle":"The origins of the universe","question":"Give one piece of scientific evidence for the Big Bang theory.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: red shift (light from distant galaxies is stretched, showing they are moving away, so the universe is expanding); cosmic microwave background radiation; the abundance of light elements such as hydrogen and helium.","evidence":["Hubble's observations (1929)","CMB radiation (1964)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"Big Bang"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-1-2","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.1","moduleTitle":"The origins of the universe","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about the Genesis account of creation.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Some literalist Christians believe the Genesis account is literally true: God created the universe in six 24-hour days only a few thousand years ago. They believe the Bible is the inspired word of God and cannot be wrong, so where science disagrees, science must be mistaken.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, most Christians and many Jews in Britain read Genesis as a non-literal story that teaches spiritual truths – that God is the creator and that the world is good – and accept the Big Bang. They point out that a 'day' in Genesis does not have to mean 24 hours, and that the Big Bang theory was first proposed by a Catholic priest, Georges Lemaître, showing that faith and science can agree.","evidence":["Genesis 1","Georges Lemaître (1927)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"creation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-1-3","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.1","moduleTitle":"The origins of the universe","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about the relationship between the Big Bang theory and religious belief.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe the Big Bang theory is compatible with belief in God. They argue that science explains how the universe began while religion explains why, so God could have used the Big Bang as the way to bring the universe into being; the Catholic Church teaches that faith and reason do not contradict each other.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, many Muslims in Britain believe the Big Bang fits with the Qur'an. They point to Qur'an 21:30, which says the heavens and earth were 'joined together' before Allah 'split them apart', and to 51:47 about the expanding heavens, as showing that scientific discoveries reveal the work of Allah the Creator.","evidence":["Catechism of the Catholic Church 159 (faith and science)","Qur'an 21:30","Qur'an 51:47"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"Big Bang"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-1-4","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.1","moduleTitle":"The origins of the universe","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about how the universe came into existence.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe God created the universe out of nothing (ex nihilo) by his word. Genesis 1:1 states 'In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth', and throughout the chapter God speaks and things come into being ('Let there be light'). This shows that God is all-powerful and that creation is deliberate and good – 'God saw all that he had made, and it was very good' (Genesis 1:31).\n\nBelief 2: Hindus believe the universe has no single beginning but goes through endless cycles of creation, preservation and destruction. Brahma creates, Vishnu preserves and Shiva destroys so that creation can begin again. The Rig Veda's Creation Hymn (Nasadiya Sukta, 10.129) even asks whether anyone truly knows how creation arose, which shows a humble and questioning approach to the origins of the universe.","evidence":["Genesis 1:1, 1:3, 1:31","Rig Veda 10.129 (Nasadiya Sukta)"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"creation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-1-5","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.1","moduleTitle":"The origins of the universe","question":"'Science has shown that God did not create the universe.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nSome atheists and humanists agree. The Big Bang theory, supported by red shift and cosmic microwave background radiation, explains the development of the universe from a hot, dense state about 13.8 billion years ago through natural laws. This contradicts a literal reading of Genesis, in which the world is made in six days a few thousand years ago. If science can explain the universe naturally, there is no need to add God as an explanation – this follows the principle that the simplest explanation is usually best (Ockham's razor). Scientists such as Stephen Hawking argued that the laws of physics could account for the universe coming into existence without a creator. So for these thinkers, science has made the idea of a creator unnecessary.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nHowever, most Christians argue that science has not shown this at all. Science explains how the universe developed but not why there is something rather than nothing, or what caused the Big Bang. The Big Bang theory itself was first proposed by Georges Lemaître, a Catholic priest, who saw no conflict with his faith. Many Christians read Genesis non-literally: its message is that God is the creator and that creation is good, not a scientific timetable. Some argue the Big Bang actually supports creation ex nihilo, because it suggests the universe had a beginning, which fits with the First Cause argument. Muslims point to Qur'an 21:30, where the heavens and the earth were 'joined together' and then split apart, as consistent with the Big Bang. The fine-tuning of the laws of physics for life is also seen by some as evidence of a designer.\n\nCONCLUSION\nScience has shown that a literal six-day creation is very unlikely, so the statement is true for literalist creationists. But science studies the natural world and cannot test something outside nature, so it cannot prove or disprove a creator God. The question of what began the Big Bang remains unanswered by science. I therefore disagree with the statement: science has changed how many believers understand creation, but it has not shown that God did not create the universe; the two can be seen as answering different questions.","evidence":["Genesis 1","Qur'an 21:30","Georges Lemaître","Stephen Hawking","Ockham's razor","Red shift and cosmic microwave background radiation"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"science and religion"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-2-0","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.2","moduleTitle":"The value of the world: stewardship, the environment and the use of animals","question":"Which one of the following is the religious idea that humans have a duty to look after the world for God?\nA Dominion\nB Stewardship\nC Awe\nD Pollution","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Stewardship.\nStewardship means caring for creation on God's behalf (Genesis 2:15). Dominion means having authority or rule over creation.","evidence":["Genesis 2:15"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"stewardship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-2-1","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.2","moduleTitle":"The value of the world: stewardship, the environment and the use of animals","question":"Give one way religious believers might reduce pollution.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: recycling; using public transport, walking or cycling; switching to renewable energy; reducing single-use plastic; campaigning through groups such as A Rocha or Christian Aid; using an eco-friendly place of worship (e.g. Eco Church scheme).","evidence":["Laudato Si' (2015)","Eco Church (A Rocha UK)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"environment"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-2-2","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.2","moduleTitle":"The value of the world: stewardship, the environment and the use of animals","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about animal experimentation.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians accept animal experimentation for medical research if it is necessary to save human lives and suffering is kept to a minimum. They believe that only humans are made in the image of God, so human life has greater value, but humans are stewards who must not be cruel ('The righteous care for the needs of their animals', Proverbs 12:10).\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many Buddhists oppose animal experimentation. The first Moral Precept is to avoid harming living beings, and because all sentient beings can suffer and may be reborn in different forms, causing pain to animals creates bad karma and goes against compassion (karuna).","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Proverbs 12:10","First Moral Precept","Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act 1986"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"animal experimentation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-2-3","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.2","moduleTitle":"The value of the world: stewardship, the environment and the use of animals","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about the duty to protect the environment.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe they have a duty of stewardship. God put Adam in the garden 'to work it and take care of it' (Genesis 2:15), and Pope Francis in Laudato Si' urged Christians to care for the earth as 'our common home', so many churches in Britain join schemes such as Eco Church to cut their carbon emissions.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, Muslims believe humans are khalifah – trustees of Allah's creation. They will be judged on the Day of Judgement on how they used the earth, so many British Muslims support projects that reduce waste and plant trees, following the hadith that planting a tree is a form of charity.","evidence":["Genesis 2:15","Laudato Si' (2015)","Qur'an 6:165","Hadith on planting trees (Bukhari)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"stewardship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-2-4","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.2","moduleTitle":"The value of the world: stewardship, the environment and the use of animals","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about the use of animals for food.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Most Christians believe it is acceptable to eat meat because God gave animals to humans for food after the flood: 'Everything that lives and moves about will be food for you' (Genesis 9:3). However, as stewards they should treat animals humanely, so some Christians choose free-range or ethically farmed food, and some choose vegetarianism.\n\nBelief 2: Many Hindus are vegetarian because of the principle of ahimsa (non-violence). The Laws of Manu (5.48) teach that meat cannot be obtained without injury to living beings, which harms one's karma. Because Hindus believe Brahman is present in all living things and the atman can be reborn in animal form, killing animals for food is seen as harming a sacred life; the cow in particular is sacred and beef is avoided.","evidence":["Genesis 9:3","Laws of Manu 5.48","Ahimsa","Atman in all beings"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"animals for food"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-2-5","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.2","moduleTitle":"The value of the world: stewardship, the environment and the use of animals","question":"'Humans have the right to use the world however they want.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nSome people might support the statement. In Genesis 1:28 God gives humans dominion – 'rule over the fish in the sea and the birds in the sky and over every living creature'. Some Christians in the past interpreted this as giving humans authority to use the earth's resources for their own benefit, for example to farm, mine and build, because humans alone are made in God's image. Some people argue that economic growth based on using natural resources has lifted billions out of poverty, and that human needs should come first. From a non-religious perspective, some argue that nature has no purpose or value except what humans give it, so it is ours to use.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMost religious believers strongly disagree. Christians emphasise stewardship: God placed Adam in Eden 'to work it and take care of it' (Genesis 2:15), and 'the earth is the Lord's' (Psalm 24:1), so humans do not own the world – they look after it for God and will be accountable. Pope Francis' Laudato Si' warns that the misuse of the earth harms the poor most and calls for 'ecological conversion'. Muslims believe they are khalifah, trustees who will answer to Allah on the Day of Judgement for their care of creation. Jewish teaching of bal tashchit forbids needless destruction, and Buddhists stress interdependence, so harming the environment harms everyone. Humanists also reject the statement: we have a responsibility to future generations, and climate change, plastic pollution and the loss of species show that using the world 'however we want' has terrible consequences. The UK's Climate Change Act 2008 reflects this idea that our use of the world must be limited.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe argument from dominion is weak, because even in Genesis dominion is balanced by the duty of care, and almost all religious and non-religious traditions agree that humans are responsible for the world. The scientific evidence of environmental damage makes the consequences of the statement clear. I therefore conclude that humans have the right to use the world's resources to meet their needs, but not 'however they want': the right comes with a responsibility of stewardship to God, to other people and to future generations.","evidence":["Genesis 1:28","Genesis 2:15","Psalm 24:1","Laudato Si' (2015)","Khalifah","Bal tashchit","Climate Change Act 2008"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"dominion and stewardship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-3-0","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.3","moduleTitle":"The origins and value of human life: evolution, sanctity of life, quality of life and abortion","question":"Which one of the following is the belief that life is holy because it is given by God?\nA Quality of life\nB Pro-choice\nC Sanctity of life\nD Evolution","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C Sanctity of life.\nSanctity of life means life is sacred because God created it (e.g. Genesis 1:27). Quality of life refers to how good or fulfilling a life is.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Jeremiah 1:5"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"sanctity of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-3-1","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.3","moduleTitle":"The origins and value of human life: evolution, sanctity of life, quality of life and abortion","question":"Give one condition under which abortion is legal in Great Britain.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: two doctors agree that continuing the pregnancy poses a greater risk to the woman's physical or mental health (up to 24 weeks); risk to the health of existing children; the woman's life is at risk; a substantial risk the child would be born with a serious disability.","evidence":["Abortion Act 1967 (amended 1990)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"abortion"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-3-2","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.3","moduleTitle":"The origins and value of human life: evolution, sanctity of life, quality of life and abortion","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about abortion.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Catholic Church teaches that abortion is always wrong because life begins at conception and is sacred. God knows each person before birth ('Before I formed you in the womb I knew you', Jeremiah 1:5), so deliberately ending a pregnancy is the killing of an innocent human being and breaks the commandment 'Do not kill'.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, Judaism teaches that abortion is permitted, and even required, when the mother's life is at risk. The Mishnah teaches that the mother's life takes priority because the fetus is not considered a full person until it is born, so the principle of saving life (pikuach nefesh) applies to the mother first.","evidence":["Jeremiah 1:5","Exodus 20:13","Mishnah Oholot 7:6","Pikuach nefesh"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"abortion"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-3-3","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.3","moduleTitle":"The origins and value of human life: evolution, sanctity of life, quality of life and abortion","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about abortion when the mother's life is at risk.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Church of England is opposed to abortion in principle, but teaches that when the mother's life is at risk abortion may be morally acceptable as the lesser of two evils. Both lives are sacred, but where both may be lost, saving the mother is the most loving choice.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, most Muslims believe abortion is allowed if the mother's life is in danger. The Islamic legal principle that the lesser of two harms should be chosen applies: the mother is already an established life with responsibilities to her family, so her life takes priority over that of the fetus.","evidence":["Church of England General Synod statements on abortion","Islamic principle of the lesser of two evils"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"abortion"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-3-4","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.3","moduleTitle":"The origins and value of human life: evolution, sanctity of life, quality of life and abortion","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about the sanctity of life.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe that human life is sacred because every person is made in the image of God. Genesis 1:27 states 'God created mankind in his own image', which means each person has a special worth and dignity that does not depend on their health or abilities. This is why many Christians believe only God has the right to end a life, and oppose abortion and euthanasia.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe that life belongs to Allah, who alone decides when it begins and ends. The Qur'an teaches that 'whoever kills a soul… it is as if he had slain mankind entirely; and whoever saves one, it is as if he had saved mankind entirely' (5:32). This shows the immense value of every life, so Muslims see protecting life – for example through medical care – as a religious duty.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Qur'an 5:32","Qur'an 17:33"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"sanctity of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-3-5","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.3","moduleTitle":"The origins and value of human life: evolution, sanctity of life, quality of life and abortion","question":"'The quality of a person's life is more important than the sanctity of life.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nHumanists and many other non-religious people agree. They argue that life matters because of the experiences, relationships and happiness it contains, so decisions about abortion should consider the quality of life of everyone involved: the woman's physical and mental health, the impact on her existing children, and, in some cases, whether a child would suffer severely. A utilitarian approach aims for the greatest happiness and least suffering, which means quality of life must be weighed. Some liberal Christians also give weight to quality of life. Using situation ethics, they argue that Jesus' command to love your neighbour means acting compassionately in each situation, for example accepting abortion when a pregnancy results from rape or threatens a woman's mental health. UK law reflects this: the Abortion Act 1967 allows abortion on grounds of risk to the woman's health.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMany religious believers argue the opposite. The Catholic Church teaches that life is sacred from conception because God creates each person ('Before I formed you in the womb I knew you', Jeremiah 1:5). A person's value comes from being made in God's image, not from their health or abilities, so judging lives by their quality risks saying some lives are worth less than others, which could lead to discrimination against disabled people. Muslims believe only Allah gives and takes life (Qur'an 5:32). Hindus, Buddhists and Sikhs follow ahimsa and see the soul or consciousness as present from early in pregnancy, so taking life creates bad karma. Supporters also argue that quality of life is hard to measure and can change: people with disabilities often report fulfilled lives.\n\nCONCLUSION\nSanctity of life gives an important protection for the vulnerable, and it is held by most religious traditions. However, even many religious believers allow exceptions, such as when the mother's life is at risk, which shows that quality of life and circumstances do affect moral decisions. I conclude that the two ideas should not be set against each other: sanctity of life should be the starting point, as it protects everyone's dignity, but in hard cases a compassionate concern for quality of life must also be considered. The statement is therefore too simple, because neither principle alone is enough.","evidence":["Jeremiah 1:5","Qur'an 5:32","Situation ethics (Joseph Fletcher)","Abortion Act 1967","Humanist and utilitarian arguments"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"quality of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-4-0","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.4","moduleTitle":"Euthanasia, death and the afterlife","question":"Which one of the following is care that relieves pain and suffering at the end of life without trying to cure the illness?\nA Euthanasia\nB Palliative care\nC Assisted suicide\nD Resurrection","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Palliative care.\nPalliative care, for example in a hospice, treats pain and symptoms and supports the dying person and their family; it does not deliberately end life.","evidence":["Hospice movement – Dame Cicely Saunders (1967)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"palliative care"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-4-1","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.4","moduleTitle":"Euthanasia, death and the afterlife","question":"Give one religious belief about life after death.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: resurrection of the body; judgement by God; heaven/paradise (jannah); hell (jahannam); purgatory; reincarnation of the atman; rebirth; reaching moksha/nibbana/mukti.","evidence":["John 11:25","Qur'an 3:185"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"afterlife"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-4-2","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.4","moduleTitle":"Euthanasia, death and the afterlife","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about euthanasia.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Catholic Church teaches that active euthanasia is always wrong because life is sacred and only God has the right to end it. Instead, it supports palliative care in hospices, so that dying people receive dignity and comfort without their lives being deliberately ended.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, some liberal Christians, such as former Archbishop of Canterbury George Carey, support assisted dying for terminally ill people. They argue that Jesus' command to love your neighbour means showing compassion and respecting a person's wish not to suffer an undignified, painful death.","evidence":["Exodus 20:13","Catechism of the Catholic Church 2277","George Carey's support for assisted dying (2014)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"euthanasia"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-4-3","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.4","moduleTitle":"Euthanasia, death and the afterlife","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about euthanasia.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians oppose euthanasia because they believe human life belongs to God. As Job says, 'The Lord gave and the Lord has taken away' (Job 1:21), so deciding when to die is seen as taking a decision that belongs to God alone.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, Muslims oppose euthanasia because Allah decides the moment of death: 'No soul can die except by Allah's permission, at a term appointed' (Qur'an 3:145). Suffering may be a test of faith, and ending life early would be like suicide, which is forbidden, so Muslims instead care for the dying in the family.","evidence":["Job 1:21","Qur'an 3:145","Qur'an 4:29"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"euthanasia"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-4-4","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.4","moduleTitle":"Euthanasia, death and the afterlife","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about life after death.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe in the resurrection and eternal life. Jesus said 'I am the resurrection and the life. The one who believes in me will live, even though they die' (John 11:25). Christians believe that, as Jesus rose from the dead, believers will be raised and judged by God, and those who have faith and have followed God will live with him in heaven. This gives hope at funerals and encourages Christians to live good lives.\n\nBelief 2: Hindus believe in reincarnation: the atman (soul) is reborn into a new body after death, in a cycle called samsara. The Bhagavad Gita (2.22) compares this to a person taking off worn-out clothes and putting on new ones. The kind of rebirth depends on karma, the results of a person's actions, and the final goal is moksha – liberation from the cycle and union with Brahman.","evidence":["John 11:25","Bhagavad Gita 2.22","Samsara, karma, moksha"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"afterlife"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-4-5","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.4","moduleTitle":"Euthanasia, death and the afterlife","question":"'Euthanasia should be made legal in the UK.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nHumanists UK and campaign groups such as Dignity in Dying argue that terminally ill, mentally competent adults should have the right to choose a dignified death. People have autonomy over their own lives, and it can be crueller to force someone to endure severe suffering than to allow them to die peacefully. At present some British people travel to Dignitas in Switzerland, which means they must die earlier than they would wish and away from home, and relatives risk prosecution. Some countries, such as the Netherlands and parts of the USA, have legal assisted dying with safeguards. Some Christians, such as George Carey, argue that compassion – loving your neighbour – supports legal assisted dying, and that it is not against God's will to end unbearable suffering.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMost religious believers oppose legalisation. Christians believe life is a sacred gift: 'The Lord gave and the Lord has taken away' (Job 1:21), and the commandment 'Do not kill' applies. Muslims believe only Allah decides when someone dies (Qur'an 3:145), and suffering can be a test. Hindus and Buddhists believe euthanasia could interfere with karma and the soul's journey. There are also non-religious arguments against: the 'slippery slope' argument suggests that once euthanasia is legal, it could be extended to people who are not terminally ill, and vulnerable or elderly people might feel pressure to die so as not to be a burden. Doctors could lose patients' trust. Many religious people say the answer is better palliative care: the hospice movement, begun by Christian Dame Cicely Saunders, shows that pain can usually be controlled and death can be dignified without deliberately ending life.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe argument from compassion and autonomy is powerful, especially for people in great pain who are clearly capable of deciding. However, the risk that vulnerable people could be pressured, and the religious belief in the sanctity of life, are serious concerns. I conclude that, on balance, the law should not allow euthanasia in general, as the statement suggests; if assisted dying were ever to be allowed it would need to be restricted to terminally ill adults with very strict safeguards, and good palliative care should be available to everyone first, so that nobody chooses death because they lack proper care.","evidence":["Job 1:21","Qur'an 3:145","Exodus 20:13","Suicide Act 1961","Dignitas","Hospice movement","Slippery slope argument"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"euthanasia"}],"paper3":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-1-0","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.1","moduleTitle":"The Design argument and the First Cause argument","question":"Which one of the following philosophers used the watchmaker analogy to argue for God's existence?\nA Bertrand Russell\nB David Hume\nC William Paley\nD Charles Darwin","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C William Paley.\nIn Natural Theology (1802) Paley compared the universe to a watch found on a heath: its complexity and purpose point to a designer. Hume and Russell criticised arguments for God; Darwin's theory of evolution is often used against the design argument.","evidence":["William Paley, Natural Theology (1802)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"design argument"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-1-1","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.1","moduleTitle":"The Design argument and the First Cause argument","question":"Give one weakness of the First Cause argument.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: if everything has a cause, what caused God?; the universe could have existed forever/an infinite chain of causes may be possible; the Big Bang could be the first cause without God; the first cause need not be the God of religion (all-loving, personal); the universe may just be a 'brute fact' (Russell).","evidence":["Bertrand Russell (1948 BBC debate with Copleston)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"first cause argument"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-1-2","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.1","moduleTitle":"The Design argument and the First Cause argument","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about the Design argument.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe the Design argument shows that God exists. The complexity of things such as the human eye, and the way the universe is fine-tuned for life, suggests a designer, just as Paley argued that a watch must have a watchmaker; Psalm 19:1 says 'The heavens declare the glory of God'.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many atheists and humanists reject the Design argument. They argue that Darwin's theory of evolution by natural selection explains how complex living things develop gradually without a designer, and that natural disasters and diseases suggest the world is not well designed by a loving God.","evidence":["William Paley's watch analogy","Psalm 19:1","Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1859)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"design argument"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-1-3","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.1","moduleTitle":"The Design argument and the First Cause argument","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about the First Cause argument.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians accept the First Cause argument. Following Thomas Aquinas, they argue that everything has a cause and there cannot be an infinite chain of causes, so there must be an uncaused first cause – God, who 'in the beginning… created the heavens and the earth' (Genesis 1:1) – and they see the Big Bang as evidence that the universe had a beginning.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many atheists argue the argument contradicts itself: if everything needs a cause, then God needs a cause too. Bertrand Russell argued that the universe might simply exist without any explanation – it is 'just there' – so there is no reason to conclude it was caused by God.","evidence":["Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologica (Five Ways)","Genesis 1:1","Bertrand Russell"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"first cause argument"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-1-4","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.1","moduleTitle":"The Design argument and the First Cause argument","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about what the natural world shows about God.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe the beauty and order of nature show that God is the creator. Paul wrote that 'since the creation of the world God's invisible qualities – his eternal power and divine nature – have been clearly seen, being understood from what has been made' (Romans 1:20). This is why many Christians see the Design argument as reasonable, and feel awe and wonder at the natural world.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe nature is full of signs (ayat) of Allah. The Qur'an says 'in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of night and day there are signs for people of understanding' (3:190). This means studying the natural world can lead people to recognise Allah's power and wisdom, and Muslims believe Allah's oneness (tawhid) is reflected in the unity and order of creation.","evidence":["Romans 1:20","Qur'an 3:190","Psalm 19:1"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"design argument"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-1-5","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.1","moduleTitle":"The Design argument and the First Cause argument","question":"'The First Cause argument proves that God exists.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany Christians and Muslims think the First Cause argument is convincing. Thomas Aquinas argued that everything we observe is caused by something else, and that a chain of causes cannot go back for ever, because then nothing would have started it. Therefore there must be a first cause that was not itself caused, and this is what people call God. This fits with Genesis 1:1, 'In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth', and with Muslim belief in Allah as al-Khaliq (the Creator). The Kalam version, developed by Muslim thinkers such as al-Ghazali, argues that whatever begins to exist has a cause, and the universe began to exist, so it has a cause. The Big Bang theory seems to support this, because it suggests that the universe had a beginning about 13.8 billion years ago. Believers argue that God is eternal, so God does not need a cause.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nAtheists and humanists argue the argument does not prove anything. If everything needs a cause, it is inconsistent to say that God does not. If God can be uncaused, why can't the universe be uncaused? Bertrand Russell argued that the universe is just a 'brute fact' – it may not need any explanation. Some scientists suggest the Big Bang may have resulted from natural processes or a previous universe, so there is no need to bring in God. Even if there were a first cause, it would not have to be the all-loving, all-powerful, personal God of Christianity or Islam – it could be an impersonal force. Some Christians also accept that the argument is not a proof: they believe in God through faith and revelation, and see the argument only as supporting what they already believe.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe First Cause argument raises a powerful question – why is there something rather than nothing? – which science has not fully answered. However, a proof should leave no room for reasonable doubt, and the argument depends on assumptions (that an infinite chain is impossible, that God is the only possible first cause) which can reasonably be questioned. I conclude that the argument does not prove that God exists, but it can make belief in God reasonable for someone who already accepts other evidence, such as religious experience or scripture.","evidence":["Thomas Aquinas' Second/Third Ways","Kalam argument (al-Ghazali)","Genesis 1:1","Bertrand Russell's 'brute fact'","Big Bang theory"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"first cause argument"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-2-0","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.2","moduleTitle":"Miracles, evil and suffering, and scientific arguments against God","question":"Which one of the following is an example of natural evil?\nA Murder\nB An earthquake\nC Terrorism\nD Theft","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B An earthquake.\nNatural evil is suffering caused by nature rather than by human choices. Murder, terrorism and theft are moral evil, caused by people misusing their free will.","evidence":["Definitions: moral evil and natural evil"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"evil and suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-2-1","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.2","moduleTitle":"Miracles, evil and suffering, and scientific arguments against God","question":"Give one example of a miracle.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: Jesus turning water into wine at Cana; Jesus healing a blind man; the raising of Lazarus; the resurrection of Jesus; a healing at Lourdes recognised by the Catholic Church; the revelation of the Qur'an to Muhammad.","evidence":["John 2:1–11","John 11","Lourdes healings"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"miracles"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-2-2","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.2","moduleTitle":"Miracles, evil and suffering, and scientific arguments against God","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about miracles.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe miracles happen and show that God is real and acts in the world. The Gospels record Jesus healing the sick and rising from the dead, and Catholics believe that healings at Lourdes, some officially recognised as medically inexplicable, show God still acts today.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many atheists and humanists believe miracles do not happen. Following David Hume, they argue it is always more likely that witnesses were mistaken, exaggerating or lying than that the laws of nature were broken, and that 'miracle cures' can be explained by science, such as spontaneous remission or the placebo effect.","evidence":["Mark 5:21–43","Lourdes Medical Bureau","David Hume, Of Miracles (1748)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"miracles"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-2-3","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.2","moduleTitle":"Miracles, evil and suffering, and scientific arguments against God","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about whether evil and suffering show that God does not exist.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many atheists believe that evil and suffering show that God does not exist. If God were all-powerful he could stop suffering, and if he were all-loving he would want to; since suffering such as childhood cancer and tsunamis exists, they conclude that such a God does not exist.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, Christians believe suffering does not disprove God. They argue that God gave humans free will, which makes real love possible but also means humans can cause moral evil, and that suffering can help people develop qualities such as compassion and courage (John Hick's soul-making theodicy).","evidence":["Epicurus / J.L. Mackie's inconsistent triad","Free will defence","John Hick's soul-making theodicy"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"evil and suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-2-4","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.2","moduleTitle":"Miracles, evil and suffering, and scientific arguments against God","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about why God allows suffering.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe that suffering entered the world through human misuse of free will. In Genesis 3, Adam and Eve disobeyed God and this 'Fall' brought sin and suffering into the world. God gives humans free will because love must be freely chosen, so he allows them to make wrong choices, which explains moral evil such as war and cruelty.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe suffering is a test from Allah. The Qur'an says 'We will surely test you with something of fear and hunger and a loss of wealth and lives and fruits, but give good tidings to the patient' (2:155). This means that hardships are an opportunity for Muslims to show patience (sabr) and trust in Allah, and they will be rewarded on the Day of Judgement for how they respond.","evidence":["Genesis 3","Qur'an 2:155–156","Job 38:4"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"evil and suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-2-5","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.2","moduleTitle":"Miracles, evil and suffering, and scientific arguments against God","question":"'Miracles prove that God exists.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany Christians believe miracles are strong evidence for God. A miracle is an event that cannot be explained by the laws of nature, so if one occurs, the best explanation is a supernatural cause – God. The Gospels record many miracles of Jesus, such as healing a man born blind (John 9) and his resurrection, which Christians see as the greatest proof of God's power. The Catholic Church has recognised around 70 healings at Lourdes after careful investigation by doctors at the Lourdes Medical Bureau found no medical explanation. For someone who experiences a miracle personally, such as a sudden recovery after prayer, the experience may be overwhelming evidence. Muslims believe the Qur'an itself is a miracle, since Muhammad could not read or write, yet the Qur'an is regarded as perfect in its language and wisdom.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nNon-religious people argue that miracles prove nothing. David Hume argued that we should always believe the more likely explanation, and it is always more likely that a witness is mistaken, deceived or lying than that a law of nature has been broken. Many miracle reports come from religious people who wanted to believe, or from long ago when science was less developed. Many apparent miracles can be explained by science, such as spontaneous remission of cancer or psychological effects. Some events are simply coincidences. A further problem is that if God performs miracles, why does he heal some people and not prevent great evils such as the Holocaust? This seems unfair. Even some Christians accept that miracles are not proof: they are 'signs' that strengthen faith for those who already believe, rather than evidence that would convince anyone.\n\nCONCLUSION\nMiracles can be powerful for people who experience them and can make belief in God seem reasonable. However, 'prove' is a strong word, and the possibility of other explanations means that miracles cannot be proof for everyone. Hume's argument that it is always more reasonable to doubt reports of law-breaking events is hard to answer. I conclude that miracles do not prove that God exists, though for believers they may be meaningful signs that support their faith.","evidence":["John 9","Resurrection of Jesus","Lourdes Medical Bureau (around 70 recognised healings)","David Hume, Of Miracles","Qur'an as miracle"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"miracles"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-3-0","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.3","moduleTitle":"Special revelation, visions and enlightenment","question":"Which one of the following is an example of special revelation?\nA Seeing God's power in a sunset\nB Reading about creation in a science book\nC Paul's vision on the road to Damascus\nD Feeling awe at the size of the universe","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C Paul's vision on the road to Damascus.\nSpecial revelation is a direct, personal experience of the divine given to a particular person (Acts 9). A and D describe general revelation through nature.","evidence":["Acts 9:1–19"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"special revelation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-3-1","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.3","moduleTitle":"Special revelation, visions and enlightenment","question":"Give one non-religious explanation for a person claiming to have seen a vision.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: hallucination; the effect of drugs or alcohol; mental or physical illness (e.g. psychosis, epilepsy, high fever); lack of sleep or food; wishful thinking; the person is lying or seeking attention; a dream mistaken for reality.","evidence":["Psychological explanations of religious experience (e.g. Freud)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"visions"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-3-2","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.3","moduleTitle":"Special revelation, visions and enlightenment","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about visions.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe visions can be genuine special revelation from God. For example, Saul saw a blinding light and heard Jesus speak to him on the road to Damascus (Acts 9), and his life was transformed as he became the apostle Paul, which they see as evidence that the vision really came from God.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many atheists and humanists believe visions are not real experiences of God but can be explained by the brain. They may be hallucinations caused by illness, drugs, tiredness or strong emotion, and the fact that people tend to see figures from their own religion suggests that visions come from the person's own mind and expectations.","evidence":["Acts 9:1–19","Psychological and neurological explanations"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"visions"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-3-3","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.3","moduleTitle":"Special revelation, visions and enlightenment","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about the problems of claimed visions.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians, including the Catholic Church, are cautious about claimed visions. The Church investigates them carefully over many years and approves very few, because some people could be mistaken or lying, and a false vision could mislead believers away from the teaching of the Bible and the Church.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, humanists believe claims of visions must be treated with caution. They argue that private experiences cannot be tested or checked by anyone else, so they cannot provide reliable knowledge about God and should not be accepted without strong evidence.","evidence":["Catholic Church norms for discerning apparitions (2024)","Humanist emphasis on evidence"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"visions"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-3-4","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.3","moduleTitle":"Special revelation, visions and enlightenment","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about special revelation as a source of knowledge about the divine.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Muslims believe the most important special revelation was the Qur'an, revealed by Allah to the Prophet Muhammad through the angel Jibril, beginning on Mount Hira in 610 CE with the words 'Read! In the name of your Lord who created' (Qur'an 96:1). Because this revelation came directly from Allah, Muslims believe the Qur'an is the final, perfect word of God and the most reliable source of knowledge about him.\n\nBelief 2: Christians believe God has revealed himself through visions to individuals at important moments. In Exodus 3 God spoke to Moses from a burning bush, revealing his name ('I am who I am') and calling Moses to lead the Israelites out of slavery. This shows that special revelation can tell people about God's nature – that he is holy and eternal – and about what God wants them to do.","evidence":["Qur'an 96:1–5","Exodus 3:1–14","Acts 9"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"special revelation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-3-5","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.3","moduleTitle":"Special revelation, visions and enlightenment","question":"'Visions are not a reliable source of knowledge about God.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany atheists and humanists agree. Visions are private experiences, so no one else can test or check them, which makes them weak evidence. There are many alternative explanations: hallucinations can be caused by drugs, alcohol, high fever, epilepsy, lack of sleep or mental illness, and people can be mistaken or even lie for attention or money. People usually see figures from their own religion – Catholics see Mary, Hindus see Krishna – which suggests that visions are shaped by the person's own mind and expectations. Different religions also report visions that seem to support contradictory beliefs, so they cannot all be true. Even Christians are careful: the Catholic Church investigates claimed visions for many years and approves very few, which shows that many claims are not considered genuine.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMany Christians argue that visions can be a genuine source of knowledge about God. The Bible contains many examples, such as Moses at the burning bush (Exodus 3) and Saul's vision on the road to Damascus (Acts 9). Saul went from persecuting Christians to becoming the apostle Paul, prepared to suffer and die for Christ; a change this dramatic is hard to explain if nothing real happened. The vision of Bernadette at Lourdes led to a place of pilgrimage where millions find faith and healing. Muslims believe the Prophet Muhammad's experience of the angel Jibril was a genuine revelation, giving the Qur'an. Philosopher Richard Swinburne's 'principle of credulity' says we should normally believe what people tell us they have experienced unless we have good reason not to, and the 'principle of testimony' says we should trust honest witnesses.\n\nCONCLUSION\nVisions can be life-changing and some, like Paul's, are hard to explain away. However, because they cannot be tested and there are many natural explanations, they are not reliable for everyone. I conclude that the statement is largely true as a general claim: visions cannot provide reliable knowledge about God that would convince a sceptic. But for the person who has the experience, and when it fits with scripture and leads to good results, a vision may reasonably be accepted as meaningful knowledge of God.","evidence":["Acts 9","Exodus 3","Qur'an 96:1","Swinburne's principles of credulity and testimony","Psychological explanations"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"visions"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-4-0","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.4","moduleTitle":"General revelation, ideas about the divine and the value of revelation","question":"Which one of the following means that God is present in and involved with the world?\nA Transcendent\nB Immanent\nC Omniscient\nD Impersonal","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Immanent.\nAn immanent God is close to and active within creation, e.g. through the Holy Spirit. Transcendent means beyond the world; omniscient means all-knowing.","evidence":["Qur'an 50:16","Incarnation of Jesus"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"nature of God"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-4-1","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.4","moduleTitle":"General revelation, ideas about the divine and the value of revelation","question":"Give one example of general revelation.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: nature/the natural world; scripture such as the Bible or Qur'an; conscience; reason; the beauty of a sunset or the birth of a child that inspires awe and wonder; worship/tradition.","evidence":["Psalm 19:1","Romans 1:20"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"general revelation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-4-2","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.4","moduleTitle":"General revelation, ideas about the divine and the value of revelation","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about nature as a source of knowledge about God.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe that nature is general revelation, showing people God's power and creativity. Psalm 19:1 says 'The heavens declare the glory of God', so experiences of awe and wonder, such as looking at the stars, can show that God is a powerful and loving creator.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many atheists believe nature reveals nothing about God. The beauty and complexity of nature are explained by science, such as evolution and physics, and nature also includes disease and natural disasters, which suggests there is no loving designer behind it.","evidence":["Psalm 19:1","Romans 1:20","Evolution by natural selection"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"nature as general revelation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-4-3","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.4","moduleTitle":"General revelation, ideas about the divine and the value of revelation","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about scripture as a source of knowledge about God.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and non-religious beliefs.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe the Bible is a reliable source of knowledge about God because it is inspired by him: 'All Scripture is God-breathed' (2 Timothy 3:16). They believe it reveals God's character and his plan, especially through the life and teachings of Jesus in the Gospels.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, humanists believe scripture is a collection of human writings that reflect the beliefs and culture of the people who wrote them. They argue it contains contradictions and some outdated moral views, so it cannot be relied on as knowledge about a God whose existence has not been shown.","evidence":["2 Timothy 3:16","Humanist approach to scripture"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"scripture as revelation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-4-4","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.4","moduleTitle":"General revelation, ideas about the divine and the value of revelation","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about the nature of God.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe God is omnipotent – all-powerful. Jesus taught that 'with God all things are possible' (Matthew 19:26), and Christians believe God showed this power by creating the universe out of nothing and raising Jesus from the dead. This means believers can trust God to keep his promises and to help them in difficult times.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe Allah is both transcendent and immanent. He is beyond anything humans can imagine – 'There is nothing like unto Him' (Qur'an 42:11) – which is why Muslims do not make pictures of Allah. Yet the Qur'an also says 'We are closer to him than his jugular vein' (50:16), which shows that Allah knows every person's thoughts and is near to anyone who prays.","evidence":["Matthew 19:26","Qur'an 42:11","Qur'an 50:16","Surah 112 (al-Ikhlas)"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"nature of God"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-4-5","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.4","moduleTitle":"General revelation, ideas about the divine and the value of revelation","question":"'Revelation is the best way to know what God is like.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany religious believers would agree. If God is transcendent – beyond the universe and human understanding – then humans could not discover what God is like by their own efforts; God must make himself known. Christians believe God revealed himself most fully in Jesus: 'Anyone who has seen me has seen the Father' (John 14:9). Through scripture, which is 'God-breathed' (2 Timothy 3:16), Christians learn that God is loving, holy and forgiving. Muslims believe the Qur'an, revealed by Allah to Muhammad through the angel Jibril, is the perfect word of God, and it describes Allah's qualities through his 99 names, such as al-Rahman (the Most Merciful). Special revelation, such as Paul's vision, can also give direct and life-changing knowledge. General revelation through nature is available to everyone: 'The heavens declare the glory of God' (Psalm 19:1).\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOthers argue revelation is not the best, or not a reliable, way. Different religions claim different revelations that seem to contradict each other: Christians believe God is a Trinity, Muslims believe Allah is strictly one, and Hindus describe Brahman in many forms. They cannot all be completely right. Visions and religious experiences can be explained by psychological causes, and people can be mistaken or lying. Scripture is interpreted in very different ways. Some believers argue that reason (such as the First Cause and Design arguments) is also a valuable route to knowing about God. Buddhists stress enlightenment through meditation, not revelation from a God. Atheists and humanists argue that we have no reliable way of knowing what God is like because there is no good evidence that God exists.\n\nCONCLUSION\nIf God exists and is transcendent, revelation seems the most likely way to learn about God, because humans could not otherwise know a being beyond the world. However, the disagreements between revelations and the possibility of other explanations mean that revelation must be tested, for example by reason, by the community of believers and by its fruits in people's lives. I conclude that, for believers, revelation is the best way to know what God is like, but it is not a certain way, and it works best alongside reason rather than instead of it.","evidence":["John 14:9","2 Timothy 3:16","Psalm 19:1","99 names of Allah","Parable of the blind men and the elephant"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"value of revelation"}],"paper4":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-1-0","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.1","moduleTitle":"Peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, violent protest and terrorism","question":"Which one of the following means restoring friendly relationships after conflict?\nA Justice\nB Reconciliation\nC Terrorism\nD Protest","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Reconciliation.\nReconciliation means making up after conflict so that relationships are restored, e.g. the work of Coventry Cathedral after the Second World War.","evidence":["Coventry Cathedral – Community of the Cross of Nails"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"reconciliation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-1-1","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.1","moduleTitle":"Peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, violent protest and terrorism","question":"Give one reason why religious believers condemn terrorism.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: it kills innocent people; it breaks the commandment 'Do not kill'/sanctity of life; it spreads fear and hatred; it goes against teachings of peace and compassion; it damages the reputation of religion; 'There is no compulsion in religion' (Qur'an 2:256).","evidence":["Exodus 20:13","Qur'an 5:32"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"terrorism"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-1-2","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.1","moduleTitle":"Peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, violent protest and terrorism","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about the use of violence.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe violence is wrong because Jesus taught 'turn the other cheek' (Matthew 5:39) and told Peter to put away his sword when he was arrested. They believe that problems should be solved through peaceful means such as dialogue and non-violent protest, following the example of Martin Luther King Jr.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many Sikhs believe violence can be justified as a last resort to defend the innocent and oppressed. Guru Gobind Singh taught that when all other means have failed it is right to take up the sword, so a Sikh may use force to protect others from injustice, but never for revenge or gain.","evidence":["Matthew 5:39","Matthew 26:52","Zafarnama of Guru Gobind Singh"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"violence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-1-3","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.1","moduleTitle":"Peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, violent protest and terrorism","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about forgiveness after conflict.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe they should forgive those who have wronged them, because Jesus taught Peter to forgive 'seventy-seven times' (Matthew 18:22) and prayed for his executioners. After Coventry Cathedral was destroyed by bombing in 1940, Provost Howard had the words 'Father forgive' carved in the ruins, and the cathedral became a centre for reconciliation with Germany.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, Muslims believe forgiveness is important because Allah is al-Ghaffar, the Forgiving. The Qur'an encourages believers to pardon others, saying 'let them pardon and overlook. Would you not like that Allah should forgive you?' (24:22), so Muslims are encouraged to seek reconciliation rather than revenge.","evidence":["Matthew 18:21–22","Luke 23:34","Coventry Cathedral 'Father forgive'","Qur'an 24:22"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"forgiveness"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-1-4","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.1","moduleTitle":"Peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, violent protest and terrorism","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about the importance of peace.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe they are called to be peacemakers. In the Sermon on the Mount Jesus said 'Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God' (Matthew 5:9). This means Christians should actively work to bring peace, for example through organisations like Pax Christi or by building understanding between communities in conflict.\n\nBelief 2: Buddhists believe peace begins in the mind and is achieved by overcoming hatred. The Dhammapada teaches 'Hatred is never appeased by hatred in this world; by non-hatred alone is hatred appeased' (verse 5). Buddhists therefore practise loving-kindness (metta) meditation to develop inner peace, which they believe leads to peaceful actions and a more peaceful world.","evidence":["Matthew 5:9","Dhammapada 5","Metta meditation"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"peace"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-1-5","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.1","moduleTitle":"Peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, violent protest and terrorism","question":"'Religious believers should never take part in violent protest.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMost religious believers agree. Christians follow Jesus' teaching to 'turn the other cheek' (Matthew 5:39) and 'love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44), and Paul wrote, 'Do not repay anyone evil for evil' (Romans 12:17). Martin Luther King Jr, a Baptist minister, achieved major civil rights changes in the USA through non-violent marches and boycotts, showing that peaceful protest can be more powerful than violence because it wins public sympathy. Buddhists follow the first Precept of non-harming, and Hindus are inspired by Gandhi's satyagraha, which helped India gain independence. Violent protest often harms innocent bystanders, police and property, and can make people turn against the cause. In the UK the right to peaceful protest is protected by law, so violence is not necessary.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOthers argue that 'never' is too strong. When a government is extremely oppressive and peaceful protest is banned or brutally crushed, some believers think violence may be the only way to achieve justice. Dietrich Bonhoeffer, a German Lutheran pastor, joined a plot to assassinate Hitler because he believed it was necessary to stop greater evil. Some Christians point to Jesus overturning the tables of the money-changers (John 2:15) as an example of righteous anger against injustice. Sikhs believe force may be used as a last resort to protect the oppressed, following Guru Gobind Singh's teaching. Some Christians in apartheid South Africa felt that armed resistance was justified because peaceful protest had been met with violence, as at Sharpeville in 1960.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe arguments for non-violence are strong, especially in a democracy like the UK where peaceful protest is legal and effective, and where violence would contradict the teachings of most religions. Examples like King and Gandhi show non-violence can succeed. However, in extreme situations of tyranny some believers have sincerely concluded that limited force is a last resort. I conclude that religious believers in Britain today should not take part in violent protest, but I would not say 'never' in every possible situation, since the duty to defend the oppressed may sometimes outweigh the duty of non-violence.","evidence":["Matthew 5:39, 5:44","Romans 12:17","John 2:15","Martin Luther King Jr","Dietrich Bonhoeffer","Gandhi's satyagraha","Human Rights Act 1998 Article 11"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"violent protest"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-2-0","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.2","moduleTitle":"Reasons for war, just war theory, holy war and pacifism","question":"Which one of the following is a criterion of the just war theory?\nA The war must be fought for religious reasons\nB The war must be a last resort\nC The war must gain new land\nD The war must be declared by a religious leader","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B The war must be a last resort.\nJust war theory requires that all peaceful means have been tried first. Fighting for religion or authorised by a religious leader (A, D) is a feature of holy war; gaining land (C) is greed, not a just cause.","evidence":["Just war theory (Augustine, Aquinas)","Catechism of the Catholic Church 2309"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"just war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-2-1","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.2","moduleTitle":"Reasons for war, just war theory, holy war and pacifism","question":"Give one reason why a country might go to war.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: greed (for land, oil or other resources); self-defence; retaliation for an attack; to protect an ally or innocent people; religious or ideological beliefs; to remove a tyrant.","evidence":["Iraq–Kuwait 1990 (greed)","Afghanistan 2001 (retaliation)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"reasons for war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-2-2","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.2","moduleTitle":"Reasons for war, just war theory, holy war and pacifism","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about pacifism.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Quakers are Christian pacifists who refuse to take part in any war. Their Peace Testimony states that all war is contrary to the spirit of Christ, because Jesus taught 'love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44), so they work for peace through mediation and campaigning instead.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, many Muslims believe that fighting can be right in self-defence. The Qur'an says 'Fight in the way of Allah those who fight you, but do not transgress' (2:190), so although Islam values peace, Muslims may be obliged to defend themselves, their community and their faith under strict rules.","evidence":["Quaker Peace Testimony (1660)","Matthew 5:44","Qur'an 2:190"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"pacifism"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-2-3","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.2","moduleTitle":"Reasons for war, just war theory, holy war and pacifism","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about the just war theory.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians, including the Catholic Church and Church of England, accept the just war theory first developed by St Augustine and Thomas Aquinas. They believe war can be justified only if it has a just cause, such as defending the innocent, and is a last resort, because sometimes using force is the only way to stop a greater evil.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, Sikhs believe a war can be justified if it is dharam yudh – a war in defence of righteousness. Like the just war theory, it must be a last resort, fought without hatred or revenge, using minimum force, and soldiers must not loot or harm civilians.","evidence":["Augustine and Aquinas","Catechism 2309","Dharam yudh (Guru Gobind Singh)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"just war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-2-4","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.2","moduleTitle":"Reasons for war, just war theory, holy war and pacifism","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about holy war.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Most Christians today reject the idea of holy war. Although popes called the Crusades in the Middle Ages, most Christians now believe that killing in God's name goes against Jesus' teaching. When Peter drew his sword at Jesus' arrest, Jesus said 'Put your sword back in its place, for all who draw the sword will die by the sword' (Matthew 26:52), which shows that the Christian faith should not be spread or defended through violence.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims distinguish between the greater jihad – the inner struggle to obey Allah – and the lesser jihad, a defensive struggle which may involve fighting. The lesser jihad must follow strict conditions: it must be in self-defence, declared by a legitimate leader, and not harm civilians. The Qur'an teaches 'Fight in the way of Allah those who fight you, but do not transgress; Allah does not love transgressors' (2:190), so attacking innocent people can never be holy war.","evidence":["Matthew 26:52","Qur'an 2:190","Greater and lesser jihad","The Crusades (1095–1291)"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"holy war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-2-5","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.2","moduleTitle":"Reasons for war, just war theory, holy war and pacifism","question":"'Religious believers should be pacifists.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany religious believers think pacifism is the only consistent response to their faith. Jesus taught 'love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you' (Matthew 5:44) and 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9). When Peter used a sword to defend him, Jesus healed the injured servant and said 'all who draw the sword will die by the sword' (Matthew 26:52). Quakers believe there is 'that of God' in everyone, so killing anyone means destroying something sacred, and their Peace Testimony rejects all war. Buddhists follow the first Precept not to harm living beings, and violence creates bad karma. Pacifists also point out that modern wars kill huge numbers of civilians, and that non-violent movements, such as Gandhi's campaign for Indian independence, have achieved change. Pacifism does not mean doing nothing: pacifists work for peace through reconciliation, aid and campaigning.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOther believers argue that pacifism can allow great evil to continue. Augustine and Aquinas developed the just war theory because they believed that loving one's neighbour sometimes means protecting them from attack. Most Christians in Britain accept that the Second World War was justified to stop the Nazis and the Holocaust. Islam teaches that Muslims may fight in self-defence (Qur'an 2:190) under strict conditions, and Sikhs believe it is a duty to defend the oppressed as a last resort, as Guru Gobind Singh taught. The Bhagavad Gita teaches that Arjuna, as a warrior, has a dharma to fight for justice. Some argue that refusing to fight while others defend you is unfair, and that the government has a duty to protect its citizens.\n\nCONCLUSION\nPacifism is a strong, principled position which reflects Jesus' teaching and the Buddhist ideal of non-harming, and pacifists play a vital role in reminding society of the cost of war. However, most religious traditions accept that force can be a last resort to protect innocent people, and it is hard to see how pacifism could have stopped threats such as Nazi Germany. I conclude that religious believers should always work for peace and be very reluctant to go to war, but that they are not obliged to be absolute pacifists; following the just war criteria is a more realistic way to apply religious love and justice.","evidence":["Matthew 5:9, 5:44, 26:52","Quaker Peace Testimony","First Buddhist Precept","Just war theory","Qur'an 2:190","Bhagavad Gita (Arjuna)"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"pacifism"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-3-0","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.3","moduleTitle":"Religion as a cause of conflict; nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction","question":"Which one of the following is the term for having weapons in order to discourage an enemy from attacking?\nA Disarmament\nB Deterrence\nC Retaliation\nD Pacifism","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Deterrence.\nNuclear deterrence means possessing nuclear weapons so that an enemy will not attack for fear of a devastating response. Disarmament means getting rid of weapons.","evidence":["UK Trident programme"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"nuclear deterrence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-3-1","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.3","moduleTitle":"Religion as a cause of conflict; nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction","question":"Give one example of a weapon of mass destruction.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from: nuclear weapons (e.g. atomic bombs); chemical weapons (e.g. sarin, mustard gas); biological weapons (e.g. anthrax); radiological 'dirty' bombs.","evidence":["Hiroshima and Nagasaki 1945"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"weapons of mass destruction"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-3-2","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.3","moduleTitle":"Religion as a cause of conflict; nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about the possession of nuclear weapons.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: The Catholic Church, led by Pope Francis, teaches that even possessing nuclear weapons is immoral. The threat to destroy whole cities, killing millions of innocent civilians, can never be justified and goes against the sanctity of life, so Catholics should work for complete nuclear disarmament.\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, some Christians and many people of other faiths accept nuclear deterrence as a lesser evil. They argue that the UK's Trident weapons are never meant to be used but help to keep the peace by discouraging attacks, so possessing them can protect innocent people.","evidence":["Pope Francis, Nagasaki address (2019)","Gaudium et Spes 80","Mutually assured destruction"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"nuclear weapons"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-3-3","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.3","moduleTitle":"Religion as a cause of conflict; nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about the use of weapons of mass destruction.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Most Christians believe using weapons of mass destruction is wrong because they cannot meet the just war criteria. They kill civilians indiscriminately and cause harm far greater than any good achieved, so they break the rules of proportionality and discrimination between soldiers and civilians.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, Buddhists oppose the use of weapons of mass destruction because they break the first Precept of not harming living beings. The suffering caused by weapons like the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, including radiation sickness lasting years, goes against the Buddhist values of compassion (karuna) and loving-kindness (metta).","evidence":["Just war: proportionality and discrimination","First Buddhist Precept","Hiroshima and Nagasaki 1945"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"weapons of mass destruction"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-3-4","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.3","moduleTitle":"Religion as a cause of conflict; nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about whether religion causes war.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe that true religion leads to peace, not war, and that people who use religion to justify violence are misusing it. Jesus taught 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9), and the prophet Isaiah looked forward to a time when people 'will beat their swords into ploughshares' (Isaiah 2:4). Christians therefore argue that conflicts such as the Troubles in Northern Ireland were mainly about politics and national identity rather than Christian teaching.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe Islam is a religion of peace and that terrorists who claim to fight for Islam are going against its teachings. The Qur'an says 'whoever kills a soul… it is as if he had slain mankind entirely' (5:32), and 'There is no compulsion in religion' (2:256), which means faith must never be forced on others through violence. British Muslim organisations regularly condemn terrorist attacks as un-Islamic.","evidence":["Matthew 5:9","Isaiah 2:4","Qur'an 5:32","Qur'an 2:256"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"religion as a cause of war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-3-5","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.3","moduleTitle":"Religion as a cause of conflict; nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction","question":"'Religion is the main cause of war in the world today.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nSome people, including atheists such as Richard Dawkins, argue that religion is a major cause of conflict. Religions make claims about absolute truth, so believers may see those who disagree as enemies. Terrorist groups such as ISIS and Al-Qaeda have used religious language to justify attacks, including in the UK. There has been violence between Hindus and Muslims in parts of India, and the Israel–Palestine conflict involves sacred places such as Jerusalem that are important to Jews, Christians and Muslims. In history, the Crusades were fought in the name of Christianity. People who hold this view argue that without religion there would be one less reason to divide humanity into 'us' and 'them'.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nHowever, many argue that this is not true. Most wars today are about land, resources, power, nationalism or ethnicity: for example, Russia's invasion of Ukraine in 2022 is not a religious war. Even conflicts that seem religious often have other causes; the Troubles in Northern Ireland were mainly about national identity and civil rights, even though the communities were labelled Catholic and Protestant. Religious teachings themselves usually promote peace: Jesus taught 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9), the Qur'an teaches 'if they incline to peace, then incline to it also' (8:61), and Buddhists follow the Precept of non-harming. Many religious people work for peace, such as Desmond Tutu in South Africa, and religious organisations care for victims of war. Some of the deadliest conflicts of the 20th century, such as the two World Wars, were mainly caused by nationalism and political ideologies rather than religion.\n\nCONCLUSION\nReligion can be misused to inflame conflict, and in some cases religious identity is an important factor. However, the evidence suggests that most wars today have political, economic or ethnic causes, and that religion is often blamed for conflicts that are really about other issues. Religion is also a major force for peacemaking. I conclude that the statement is not justified: religion is sometimes a contributing factor, but it is not the main cause of war in the world today.","evidence":["Matthew 5:9","Qur'an 8:61","Richard Dawkins","Northern Ireland Troubles","Russia–Ukraine war (2022–)","Desmond Tutu"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"religion as a cause of war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-4-0","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.4","moduleTitle":"Religion and peace-making; responses to the victims of war","question":"Which one of the following was a Christian leader who chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission?\nA Martin Luther King Jr\nB Desmond Tutu\nC Mahatma Gandhi\nD Thich Nhat Hanh","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B Desmond Tutu.\nArchbishop Desmond Tutu, an Anglican, chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission (1996–98) after the end of apartheid, based on his belief in forgiveness and reconciliation.","evidence":["Truth and Reconciliation Commission (1996–98)"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"peace-making"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-4-1","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.4","moduleTitle":"Religion and peace-making; responses to the victims of war","question":"Give one way in which a religious organisation helps the victims of war.","marks":1,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any one from, for example: providing emergency food, water and shelter; medical care; helping refugees resettle; counselling for trauma; rebuilding homes, schools and hospitals; campaigning for peace and refugee rights; reuniting families.","evidence":["Christian Aid","Islamic Relief","Khalsa Aid"],"examTip":"Multiple-choice: read all four options before choosing; only one letter is credited.","answerTopic":"victims of war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-4-2","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.4","moduleTitle":"Religion and peace-making; responses to the victims of war","question":"Explain two similar beliefs in contemporary British society about helping victims of war.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe they should help victims of war because Jesus taught in the parable of the Good Samaritan that everyone in need is our neighbour, even a stranger or enemy. This is why Christian organisations such as Christian Aid provide shelter, food and long-term support to refugees from conflicts.\n\nBelief 2: Similarly, Sikhs believe that sewa (selfless service) to anyone in need is a way of serving God. Khalsa Aid, a Sikh charity founded in the UK, takes food, water and emergency supplies to people affected by war, such as refugees in Syria and Ukraine, whatever their religion.","evidence":["Luke 10:25–37","Christian Aid","Khalsa Aid (founded 1999)"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"victims of war"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-4-3","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.4","moduleTitle":"Religion and peace-making; responses to the victims of war","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about how religious believers should respond to conflict.\nIn your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Many Christians believe the right response is active peace-making through reconciliation. For example, the Corrymeela Community in Northern Ireland brings Catholics and Protestants together to talk and pray, because Jesus said 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9).\n\nBelief 2: In contrast, some Sikhs believe that, as well as working for peace, believers may sometimes have a duty to take up arms to defend the oppressed. Guru Gobind Singh created the Khalsa as saint-soldiers who must protect the weak, so Sikhs see defending others as a last resort as part of their response to conflict.","evidence":["Matthew 5:9","Corrymeela Community","Khalsa – saint-soldiers"],"examTip":"Two different (or similar) beliefs, each developed with a second sentence of detail. You must use Christianity plus another religious tradition (Theme C: Christianity plus non-religious beliefs). No quotation needed.","answerTopic":"peace-making"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-4-4","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.4","moduleTitle":"Religion and peace-making; responses to the victims of war","question":"Explain two religious beliefs about why believers should work for peace.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":6,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Belief 1: Christians believe they should work for peace because Jesus taught 'Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God' (Matthew 5:9). This inspired Archbishop Desmond Tutu, who believed that forgiveness and reconciliation were essential for a peaceful South Africa after apartheid. He led the Truth and Reconciliation Commission so that the truth about the past could be told and enemies could learn to live together.\n\nBelief 2: Muslims believe they should work for peace because Allah commands it: 'If they incline to peace, then incline to it also, and trust in Allah' (Qur'an 8:61). The word Islam is related to salaam, meaning peace, and Muslims greet each other with 'peace be upon you'. Organisations such as Islamic Relief support peace-building and help civilians in conflict zones because Muslims believe every human being is created by Allah and deserves dignity and protection.","evidence":["Matthew 5:9","Qur'an 8:61","Desmond Tutu","Islamic Relief"],"examTip":"Two beliefs, each developed, plus a named sacred text or source of authority that you apply to the question. Naming the source and showing what it means for the issue gets both source marks.","answerTopic":"peace-making"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-4-5","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.4","moduleTitle":"Religion and peace-making; responses to the victims of war","question":"'Religious organisations do more to help victims of war than anyone else.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you:\n• should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement\n• should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• should refer to religious arguments\n• may refer to non-religious arguments\n• should reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThere is strong evidence that religious organisations play a major role in helping victims of war. Christian Aid, CAFOD, Tearfund, Islamic Relief, Khalsa Aid and World Jewish Relief provide food, water, medical care and shelter in conflict zones such as Syria, Yemen, Gaza and Ukraine. Religious teaching motivates believers to give: Christians follow the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10) and Jesus' words 'whatever you did for one of the least of these… you did for me' (Matthew 25:40); Muslims give zakat and sadaqah; Sikhs perform sewa. Religious organisations often have local networks of churches, mosques and gurdwaras, so they can reach people quickly and stay for the long term, helping with trauma counselling and rebuilding communities. Religious individuals such as Desmond Tutu have also helped heal the wounds of war through reconciliation.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nHowever, many non-religious organisations do enormous work too. The International Committee of the Red Cross, Médecins Sans Frontières (Doctors Without Borders), Save the Children and UN agencies such as UNHCR, which supports millions of refugees, are not religious. Governments give billions of pounds in aid and can provide peacekeeping forces, which charities cannot. Humanists argue that compassion is a human value that does not depend on religion, and many non-religious people volunteer and donate generously. It is also very difficult to measure who does 'more' – religious and non-religious groups often work together. Some critics argue that religion can also be part of the cause of conflicts, so it should not be given all the credit for responding to them.\n\nCONCLUSION\nReligious organisations clearly make a huge and valuable contribution, inspired by teachings of love, justice and service. However, the largest aid organisations, such as UN agencies and the Red Cross, are not religious, and governments have resources that charities cannot match. I conclude that the statement is not justified: religious organisations do a great deal and may be especially good at long-term, local support, but they are one part of a wider effort, and helping victims of war is shared by people of all faiths and none.","evidence":["Luke 10:25–37","Matthew 25:40","Christian Aid, Islamic Relief, Khalsa Aid","Red Cross, MSF, UNHCR"],"examTip":"Argue for and against with logical chains of reasoning, use specific religious teaching (you are capped at Level 2 without religion), weigh the arguments and finish with a justified conclusion. Up to 3 extra SPaG marks: use specialist terms accurately.","answerTopic":"victims of war"}]},"notes":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-1","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.1","title":"Human sexuality and sexual relationships before and outside marriage","specFocus":"Human sexuality, including heterosexual and homosexual relationships; sexual relationships before and outside of marriage. Contrasting beliefs (Christianity + another religion) are required on sexual relationships before marriage and homosexual relationships.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nHuman sexuality: how people express themselves as sexual beings. Heterosexual: attracted to the opposite sex. Homosexual: attracted to the same sex. Chastity: not having sex before marriage. Adultery: a married person having sex with someone who is not their spouse. Promiscuity: having many casual sexual partners. Celibacy: choosing not to have sex at all (e.g. Catholic priests, monks and nuns).\n\nCHRISTIANITY\nTraditional teaching is that sex is a gift from God meant for marriage between a man and a woman: \"a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and they become one flesh\" (Genesis 2:24). Sex unites the couple and is open to new life. Paul calls the body \"a temple of the Holy Spirit\" (1 Corinthians 6:19) and warns against sexual immorality. Adultery breaks the seventh commandment, \"You shall not commit adultery\" (Exodus 20:14), and Jesus taught that even lustful looks are adultery in the heart (Matthew 5:28).\nOn homosexuality there are real differences. The Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that homosexual people \"must be accepted with respect, compassion and sensitivity\" but that homosexual acts are not in keeping with natural law. Many evangelical Christians point to texts such as Leviticus 18:22 and Romans 1. Liberal Christians, and denominations such as the Methodist Church and Scottish Episcopal Church (which now permit same-sex marriage), argue that the key principle is faithful, loving commitment and the command to \"love your neighbour\". The Church of England allows prayers of blessing for same-sex couples (from 2023) but does not marry them.\nSome Christians accept sex before marriage in a committed, loving relationship that is heading towards marriage; others hold that it is always wrong.\n\nOTHER RELIGIONS\nIslam: sex is only permitted within marriage (nikah). Zina (sex outside marriage) is forbidden: \"Do not go near adultery; it is an outrage and an evil path\" (Qur'an 17:32). Most Muslims regard homosexual acts as sinful (the story of Lut, e.g. Qur'an 7:80–81), though they stress that people should not be abused; some liberal Muslims (e.g. Imaan, a UK LGBT Muslim group) disagree.\nJudaism: sex within marriage is a mitzvah and joyful; the Talmud says a husband has a duty to satisfy his wife. Orthodox Judaism forbids sex before marriage and homosexual acts (Leviticus 18:22); Reform and Liberal Judaism accept same-sex relationships and conduct same-sex weddings.\nBuddhism: the third Moral Precept is to avoid sexual misconduct, i.e. sex that harms others through lying, exploitation or betrayal. Many Buddhists judge a relationship by intention and harm rather than by gender or marital status.\nHinduism and Sikhism traditionally teach that sex belongs within marriage; lust (kaam) is one of the five thieves in Sikhism.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists believe consenting adults should be free to make their own choices, as long as no one is harmed or exploited; they support LGBT equality.\n\nUK LAW AND SOCIETY\nHomosexual acts were partially decriminalised in 1967 (England and Wales); the Equality Act 2010 protects sexual orientation; same-sex marriage became legal in England and Wales in 2014. The age of consent is 16. Sex before marriage is now widely accepted in British society, and cohabitation is common."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-2","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.2","title":"Contraception and family planning","specFocus":"Contraception and family planning. Contrasting beliefs on contraception (Christianity + one or more other religious traditions) are a named requirement.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nContraception: methods used to prevent pregnancy. Artificial contraception: man-made methods such as condoms, the pill, implants, IUDs. Natural family planning (NFP): avoiding sex during the woman's fertile time, without artificial methods. Family planning: deciding when to have children and how many. Emergency contraception ('morning-after pill'): taken after sex; some believers regard methods that may stop a fertilised egg implanting as a form of early abortion.\n\nWHY PEOPLE USE CONTRACEPTION\nTo space children, protect the mother's health, for financial reasons, to avoid passing on genetic conditions, to prevent sexually transmitted infections (condoms), or because a couple does not want children.\n\nCHRISTIANITY\nCatholic: Humanae Vitae (Pope Paul VI, 1968) teaches that every sexual act within marriage must remain open to the transmission of life, so artificial contraception is wrong because it goes against natural law and God's purpose for sex. Catholics may use natural family planning for serious reasons, because it works with the natural cycle. Some Catholics in Britain disagree with this teaching in practice.\nOrthodox Churches generally discourage contraception but allow some methods with a priest's guidance.\nAnglican and most Protestant Churches: the Lambeth Conference of 1930 accepted the use of other methods in the light of Christian principles. Most Protestants today see responsible family planning as part of good stewardship of the family; they would reject methods that act after conception if they regard life as beginning at conception.\nGenesis 1:28 \"Be fruitful and increase in number\" is often quoted to show that children are a blessing.\n\nISLAM\nMost Muslims accept contraception within marriage for reasons such as the mother's health or spacing children, as long as it is not used to avoid having children altogether. Hadith report that some Companions practised coitus interruptus ('azl) in the Prophet's time and it was not forbidden. Methods that cause an early abortion are generally rejected. Some Muslims oppose contraception, trusting that Allah provides: \"Do not kill your children for fear of poverty; We will provide for them and for you\" (Qur'an 17:31).\n\nJUDAISM\nHaving children fulfils the first mitzvah, \"be fruitful and multiply\". Orthodox Judaism generally permits contraception for health reasons, usually preferring methods used by the woman (such as the pill) over condoms, which are seen as 'wasting seed'. Reform and Liberal Jews leave family planning to the couple's conscience.\n\nHINDUISM, SIKHISM, BUDDHISM\nHindus see having children as part of the grihastha (householder) stage; contraception is usually permitted, but ahimsa rules out methods that destroy a fertilised egg. Sikhism has no ban on contraception; it is a matter for the couple. Buddhists generally accept contraception that prevents conception, but not methods that harm a life once conceived (first Precept – non-harming).\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists support contraception as a responsible way of preventing unwanted pregnancies and STIs, reducing abortions, and giving women control over their lives.\n\nUK CONTEXT\nContraception is free on the NHS; under-16s can receive confidential advice (the Gillick/Fraser guidelines)."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-3","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.3","title":"Marriage, same-sex marriage, cohabitation, divorce and remarriage","specFocus":"The nature and purpose of marriage; same-sex marriage and cohabitation; divorce, including reasons for divorce, and remarrying; ethical arguments related to divorce, including those based on the sanctity of marriage vows and compassion.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nMarriage: a legally recognised union between two people; for many believers a sacred covenant made before God. Cohabitation: a couple living together in a sexual relationship without being married. Same-sex marriage: marriage between two people of the same sex (legal in England and Wales since 2014, Scotland 2014, Northern Ireland 2020). Divorce: legal ending of a marriage. Annulment: a declaration that a valid marriage never existed. Remarriage: marrying again after divorce or the death of a spouse. Sanctity of marriage vows: the belief that the promises made are sacred.\n\nPURPOSE OF MARRIAGE\nCommon religious purposes: lifelong companionship and love, a stable place for sex and for bringing up children, and a commitment made before God and the community. The Church of England marriage service lists the gift of children, companionship and a life of mutual love and support.\n\nCHRISTIANITY\nMarriage is a covenant (Catholics: a sacrament) – \"what God has joined together, let no one separate\" (Mark 10:9). Vows are made \"till death us do part\". The Catholic Church does not recognise divorce; a marriage can only be annulled (e.g. if one partner did not freely consent). Divorced Catholics who remarry civilly are not normally able to receive communion. The Church of England accepts that marriages sometimes fail and, since 2002, allows remarriage in church at the priest's discretion. Many Protestants note Jesus' exception for \"sexual immorality\" (Matthew 19:9) and stress compassion and forgiveness. The Church of England and Catholic Church do not conduct same-sex marriages; the Methodist Church (2021) and some others do. Cohabitation is traditionally seen as falling short of the commitment of marriage, though many churches welcome cohabiting couples.\n\nISLAM\nMarriage (nikah) is a contract and a duty; the Prophet said \"marriage is my sunnah\". Divorce (talaq) is allowed but \"of all lawful things, the most hated by Allah is divorce\" (hadith). There is a three-month waiting period (iddah) to allow reconciliation, and families are expected to help: \"appoint an arbiter from his family and one from hers\" (Qur'an 4:35). Remarriage is permitted.\n\nJUDAISM\nMarriage (kiddushin – 'making holy') is a sacred covenant. Divorce is allowed through a get (document of divorce) given by the husband before a Beth Din; Deuteronomy 24:1 allows it. Without a get, Orthodox Jews cannot remarry religiously. Reform Judaism is more flexible and conducts same-sex marriages.\n\nHINDUISM AND SIKHISM\nHindu marriage is a lifelong sacramental bond and divorce is traditionally discouraged, though allowed in law. In Sikhism the Anand Karaj makes the couple \"one light in two bodies\"; divorce is discouraged but not forbidden, and the community tries to reconcile couples.\n\nETHICAL ARGUMENTS ABOUT DIVORCE\nSanctity of vows: promises made before God should not be broken; divorce harms children and weakens commitment. Compassion: forcing people to stay in abusive or loveless marriages causes suffering; Jesus showed mercy to the woman caught in adultery (John 8). Humanists support divorce when a relationship has broken down and want separation handled kindly.\n\nUK LAW\nThe Divorce, Dissolution and Separation Act 2020 introduced \"no-fault\" divorce in England and Wales from April 2022. Religious organisations are not required to conduct same-sex weddings (Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013 'quadruple lock')."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper1-a-4","paper":"Theme A","module":"A.4","title":"Families and gender equality","specFocus":"The nature of families (role of parents and children; extended and nuclear families); the purpose of families (procreation, stability and protection of children, educating children in a faith); contemporary family issues (same-sex parents, polygamy); the roles of men and women; gender equality; gender prejudice and discrimination, including examples.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nNuclear family: two parents and their children. Extended family: parents, children and other relatives (grandparents, aunts, uncles) living together or close by. Single-parent family; blended (step) family; same-sex parents. Polygamy: having more than one husband or wife at the same time (polygyny = more than one wife). Procreation: having children. Gender equality: men and women having equal rights, status and opportunities. Gender prejudice: unfair judgements about someone because of their sex; discrimination: acting on that prejudice.\n\nPURPOSE OF FAMILIES\nProcreation; stability and protection of children; educating children in a faith (e.g. infant baptism and Christian upbringing; the Shema in Judaism tells parents to teach God's words \"to your children\", Deuteronomy 6:7; Muslim parents whisper the adhan into a newborn's ear).\nRoles: Christian children should \"honour your father and your mother\" (Exodus 20:12); parents should not \"exasperate your children\" but bring them up \"in the training and instruction of the Lord\" (Ephesians 6:4). In Hinduism and Sikhism the extended family is very important and caring for elderly parents is a duty.\n\nCONTEMPORARY ISSUES\nSame-sex parents: adoption by same-sex couples has been legal in England and Wales since 2005. Liberal Christians, Reform Jews and humanists argue that a loving, stable home matters most. The Catholic Church teaches that children have a right to a mother and a father. Many Muslims and Orthodox Jews hold similar traditional views.\nPolygamy: illegal in the UK (bigamy). Christianity teaches monogamy (\"the two will become one flesh\"). The Qur'an permits a man up to four wives only if he can treat them all justly, but adds \"you will never be able to deal justly between wives\" (4:3, 4:129); most British Muslims are monogamous. Polygamy was common among Old Testament figures such as Abraham and Jacob, but Judaism banned it for Ashkenazi Jews about 1000 CE.\n\nROLES OF MEN AND WOMEN AND EQUALITY\nChristianity: Genesis 1:27 says God created humankind \"male and female\", both in God's image; \"there is neither male nor female… you are all one in Christ Jesus\" (Galatians 3:28). Some Christians hold complementary roles (e.g. Ephesians 5:22 – wives submit to husbands), while most British Christians support equal roles. The Church of England has ordained women priests since 1994 and bishops since 2014; the Catholic Church ordains only men.\nIslam: men and women are equal before Allah (Qur'an 33:35) but often have complementary roles; men are expected to provide financially (4:34), and women have the right to work, own property and inherit. Khadijah, the Prophet's wife, was a successful businesswoman.\nJudaism: Orthodox Jews see different but equal roles, with the mother central to a Jewish home; Reform Judaism has women rabbis.\nSikhism: Guru Nanak taught \"From woman, man is born… why call her bad, from whom kings are born?\" (Guru Granth Sahib, Ang 473). Women can perform all roles in the gurdwara.\nBuddhism: the Buddha accepted women into the sangha as nuns (bhikkhunis).\n\nDISCRIMINATION IN THE UK\nThe Equal Pay Act 1970, Sex Discrimination Act 1975 and Equality Act 2010 outlaw sex discrimination; yet a gender pay gap and under-representation of women in leadership remain. Humanists campaign for full equality and for religions to treat women equally."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-1","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.1","title":"The origins of the universe","specFocus":"Religious teachings about the origins of the universe and different interpretations of these; the relationship between scientific views, such as the Big Bang theory, and religious views.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nCreation: the act of bringing the universe into being. Ex nihilo: 'out of nothing'. Big Bang theory: the scientific theory that the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago from an extremely hot, dense point and has been expanding ever since (evidence: red shift showing galaxies moving apart, discovered by Edwin Hubble; cosmic microwave background radiation, discovered 1964). Literalist (fundamentalist): reads scripture as literally, historically true. Non-literal (liberal): reads it as conveying spiritual truth through symbol and story.\n\nCHRISTIANITY\nGenesis 1 describes God creating in six days by speaking (\"And God said, 'Let there be light'\") and resting on the seventh; Genesis 2 tells of Adam formed from dust. John 1:1–3 says \"In the beginning was the Word… Through him all things were made\". Literalist (young-earth) creationists believe the universe was created in six 24-hour days only thousands of years ago. Most Christians in Britain, including the Catholic Church and Church of England, read Genesis non-literally and accept the Big Bang: God is the cause behind it, and science explains how, religion explains why. The Big Bang theory was first proposed by Georges Lemaître, a Catholic priest, in 1927.\n\nISLAM\nAllah is the creator of everything: \"Your Lord is God, who created the heavens and the earth in six days\" (Qur'an 7:54), where 'days' may mean long periods. Some Muslims see the Big Bang described in \"the heavens and the earth were joined together, then We split them apart\" (21:30) and the expansion of the universe in 51:47.\n\nHINDUISM\nThe universe goes through endless cycles of creation, preservation and destruction (Brahma, Vishnu, Shiva). The Nasadiya Sukta (Rig Veda 10.129) asks whether even the gods know how creation began. Many Hindus see the Big Bang as fitting a cyclical universe.\n\nJUDAISM\nShares the Genesis account; many Jews read it non-literally. Orthodox Jews may follow the traditional calendar, while others accept scientific dating.\n\nBUDDHISM\nThe Buddha did not teach about how the universe began, calling such questions 'unanswerable' and unhelpful for ending suffering (the parable of the poisoned arrow).\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nAtheists and humanists accept the Big Bang as the best evidence-based explanation, and see no need for a creator; some, such as Stephen Hawking, argued that the laws of physics could explain the universe's beginning without God. Others ask, \"what caused the Big Bang?\" – which many religious people answer with God (links to the First Cause argument, Theme C).\n\nARE SCIENCE AND RELIGION IN CONFLICT?\nConflict view: literalist creationists and some atheists. Compatibility view: most Christians and many others – science answers 'how', religion answers 'why', and the order of Genesis 1 (light, then sea, then land, then life, then humans) is broadly similar to scientific order."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-2","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.2","title":"The value of the world: stewardship, the environment and the use of animals","specFocus":"The value of the world and the duty of human beings to protect it, including religious teaching about stewardship, dominion, responsibility, awe and wonder; the use and abuse of the environment, including natural resources and pollution; the use and abuse of animals, including animal experimentation and the use of animals for food. Contrasting beliefs on animal experimentation are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nStewardship: the duty to look after the world on God's behalf. Dominion: authority or rule over creation. Awe and wonder: amazement at the beauty and complexity of nature, which for believers points to God. Natural resources: materials from the earth (oil, coal, water, timber); non-renewable resources run out. Pollution: contamination of land, air or water. Sustainable development: meeting today's needs without harming future generations. Animal experimentation: using animals in scientific research, e.g. testing medicines.\n\nCHRISTIANITY\nGod gave humans dominion: \"Rule over the fish… the birds… and over every living creature\" (Genesis 1:28), but also placed Adam in Eden \"to work it and take care of it\" (Genesis 2:15) – stewardship. \"The earth is the Lord's, and everything in it\" (Psalm 24:1). Pope Francis' encyclical Laudato Si' (2015) calls the earth \"our common home\" and urges action on climate change and care for the poor who suffer most from pollution. Christian organisations such as A Rocha and Christian Aid campaign on environmental issues. Animals: most Christians accept that animals may be used for food (Genesis 9:3) and for research that saves human lives, if suffering is minimised; humans alone are made in God's image. Some Christians are vegetarian and oppose cruelty: \"The righteous care for the needs of their animals\" (Proverbs 12:10).\n\nISLAM\nHumans are khalifah (vice-gerents/trustees) of the earth: \"It is He who has made you successors upon the earth\" (Qur'an 6:165). On the Day of Judgement people will be accountable for how they treated creation. The Prophet taught kindness to animals (the hadith of the man who gave water to a thirsty dog and was forgiven). Halal slaughter requires the animal to be treated well. Animal experimentation for genuine medical need is generally accepted if there is no unnecessary suffering; testing cosmetics is not.\n\nHINDUISM, BUDDHISM, SIKHISM, JUDAISM\nHindus see Brahman in all living things; ahimsa leads many to vegetarianism, and the cow is sacred. The Chipko movement (India, 1970s) protected trees. Buddhists follow the first Precept (do not harm living beings) and believe in interdependence; many are vegetarian and oppose animal testing. Sikhs believe God is present in creation; the Sikh calendar has an environment day. Judaism teaches bal tashchit ('do not destroy', from Deuteronomy 20:19) and tza'ar ba'alei chayim (preventing animal suffering).\n\nABUSE OF THE ENVIRONMENT\nClimate change from burning fossil fuels, deforestation, plastic pollution in oceans, overuse of resources. Responses: recycling, renewable energy, reducing consumption.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists value the natural world for its own sake and for future generations; many support animal research where needed but campaign for the '3Rs' (replace, reduce, refine). Peter Singer argues that causing animal suffering (speciesism) is wrong.\n\nUK LAW\nThe Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act 1986 regulates experiments; testing cosmetics on animals has been banned in the UK since 1998. The Climate Change Act 2008 (net zero by 2050)."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-3","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.3","title":"The origins and value of human life: evolution, sanctity of life, quality of life and abortion","specFocus":"Religious teachings about the origins of human life and different interpretations of these; the relationship between scientific views such as evolution and religious views; the concepts of sanctity of life and quality of life; abortion, including when the mother's life is at risk; ethical arguments related to abortion, including those based on sanctity of life and quality of life. Contrasting beliefs on abortion are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nEvolution: the development of species over millions of years through natural selection (Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species, 1859). Sanctity of life: the belief that life is sacred or holy because it is given by God. Quality of life: how good or fulfilling a life is, e.g. in terms of health, happiness and ability to function. Abortion: the deliberate ending of a pregnancy so that it does not result in a birth. Pro-life: believing abortion is wrong. Pro-choice: believing a woman has the right to choose.\n\nORIGINS OF HUMAN LIFE\nGenesis 1:27: \"God created mankind in his own image\"; Genesis 2:7: God formed man \"from the dust of the ground and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life\". Literalist creationists reject evolution. Most Christians (Catholic Church, Church of England) accept evolution as the way God created, but believe humans have a unique soul. Muslims believe Allah created Adam from clay (Qur'an 15:26); some accept evolution for other species but not humans. Atheists such as Richard Dawkins see evolution as making a creator unnecessary.\n\nSANCTITY OF LIFE\nChristianity: life is a gift from God – \"Before I formed you in the womb I knew you\" (Jeremiah 1:5); \"Do not kill\" (Exodus 20:13); the body is a temple of the Holy Spirit. Islam: \"whoever kills a soul… it is as if he had slain mankind entirely\" (Qur'an 5:32); only Allah gives and takes life. Judaism: saving a life (pikuach nefesh) overrides almost all other laws. Hindus, Buddhists and Sikhs: ahimsa; the atman/consciousness has value.\n\nABORTION – UK LAW\nThe Abortion Act 1967 (amended 1990) allows abortion up to 24 weeks if two doctors agree that continuing the pregnancy poses a greater risk to the physical or mental health of the woman or existing children; beyond 24 weeks if the woman's life is at serious risk or there is a substantial risk of serious disability. Different law applies in Northern Ireland (decriminalised 2019).\n\nRELIGIOUS VIEWS ON ABORTION\nCatholic Church: life begins at conception; direct abortion is always wrong. The principle of double effect allows treatment to save the mother (e.g. removing an ectopic pregnancy) even if the fetus dies as a foreseen but unintended result. Church of England: strongly opposes abortion in principle but accepts it can be the lesser of two evils in some cases (e.g. risk to the mother's life). Many liberal Christians stress compassion (situation ethics, 'love your neighbour'). Islam: most scholars allow abortion before ensoulment (often said to be 120 days) for serious reasons and always when the mother's life is at risk; many Muslims oppose it except to save the mother. Judaism: the mother's life comes first; the fetus is not regarded as a full person until birth, so abortion is permitted to save her life or health. Buddhism: abortion breaks the first Precept but intention and compassion matter. Hinduism and Sikhism generally oppose abortion except to save the mother; Sikhs are particularly opposed to sex-selective abortion.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists base decisions on quality of life and the woman's autonomy: the woman's rights, health and circumstances outweigh those of an early fetus, which cannot feel pain or have interests in the way a person does. Utilitarians weigh the happiness and suffering of all involved."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper2-b-4","paper":"Theme B","module":"B.4","title":"Euthanasia, death and the afterlife","specFocus":"Euthanasia; beliefs about death and an afterlife, and their impact on beliefs about the value of human life. Contrasting beliefs on euthanasia are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nEuthanasia: ending a life to relieve suffering ('good death'). Voluntary euthanasia: at the person's own request. Non-voluntary: when the person cannot ask (e.g. in a coma). Active: deliberately doing something to end life (e.g. a lethal injection). Passive: withdrawing or withholding treatment so that the person dies naturally. Assisted suicide (assisted dying): helping someone end their own life. Palliative care: care that relieves pain and symptoms at the end of life (e.g. in a hospice). Afterlife: life after death – heaven, hell, resurrection, reincarnation, rebirth.\n\nUK LAW\nEuthanasia and assisted suicide are illegal in England and Wales (Suicide Act 1961: assisting suicide carries up to 14 years in prison). Doctors may withdraw futile treatment, and may give pain relief that may shorten life (the principle of double effect). Some British people travel to Dignitas in Switzerland. Parliament has repeatedly debated assisted dying for terminally ill adults; check the current position, as the Terminally Ill Adults (End of Life) Bill was debated in 2024–25.\n\nCHRISTIANITY\nMost Christians oppose active euthanasia: life is a gift from God and only God should decide when it ends (Job 1:21 \"The Lord gave and the Lord has taken away\"); \"Do not kill\" (Exodus 20:13). The Catholic Church and Church of England oppose assisted suicide but accept that excessive or burdensome treatment can be refused, and strongly support hospices – the modern hospice movement was founded by Dame Cicely Saunders, a Christian, at St Christopher's Hospice (1967). Some liberal Christians (e.g. former Archbishop George Carey) support assisted dying out of compassion.\n\nISLAM\nLife belongs to Allah: \"No soul can die except by Allah's permission\" (Qur'an 3:145). Suicide and euthanasia are forbidden; suffering may be a test. Turning off life support when there is no hope of recovery may be acceptable.\n\nJUDAISM\nLife is precious; hastening death is forbidden, but it is permitted to remove something that is preventing a natural death.\n\nHINDUISM, BUDDHISM, SIKHISM\nHindus and Buddhists believe euthanasia may disturb karma and the soul's journey; a Buddhist's state of mind at death matters. Some Hindus accept the idea of a 'good death' and the tradition of prayopavesa (fasting to death) for the elderly. Sikhs believe life is God's gift and suffering should be accepted with God's will (hukam); they oppose euthanasia.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists support assisted dying for terminally ill, mentally competent adults, with safeguards: people should have autonomy and the right to a dignified death. Opponents (including some non-religious disability campaigners) fear a 'slippery slope' and pressure on vulnerable people.\n\nAFTERLIFE BELIEFS AND THE VALUE OF LIFE\nChristianity: resurrection, judgement, heaven and hell (\"I am the resurrection and the life\", John 11:25); Catholics also believe in purgatory. Islam: Akhirah – Day of Judgement, paradise (jannah) and hell (jahannam). Judaism: focus is on this life; some believe in resurrection and Olam Ha-Ba (the world to come). Hindus: samsara, reincarnation, moksha. Buddhists: rebirth, nibbana. Sikhs: reincarnation and mukti. Atheists/humanists: there is no afterlife, so this life is all we have and should be lived well.\nImpact: belief in judgement encourages moral behaviour and gives hope; belief that life continues may make death less feared; humanists argue the lack of an afterlife makes this life more precious."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-1","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.1","title":"The Design argument and the First Cause argument","specFocus":"The Design argument, including its strengths and weaknesses; the First Cause argument, including its strengths and weaknesses. (For 4-mark questions in Theme C, contrast Christianity with non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism.)","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nTheist: believes in God. Atheist: believes there is no God. Agnostic: believes it is not possible to know whether God exists. Design argument (teleological argument): the universe shows signs of design, so it must have a designer – God. First Cause argument (cosmological argument): everything has a cause, so there must be a first, uncaused cause – God. Fine-tuning: the physical constants of the universe are precisely set for life to exist.\n\nTHE DESIGN ARGUMENT\nWilliam Paley (1802), the watchmaker analogy: if you found a watch on a heath, its intricate parts working together for a purpose would show it had a designer; the universe (e.g. the human eye) is far more complex, so it too must have a designer – God. Isaac Newton is said to have argued that the thumb alone convinced him of God's existence. Thomas Aquinas (Fifth Way): things without intelligence, like planets, act towards a purpose, so they must be directed by an intelligent being. F.R. Tennant's anthropic principle: the universe was perfectly suited for human life to develop, which suggests design. Scripture: \"The heavens declare the glory of God\" (Psalm 19:1); \"Since the creation of the world God's invisible qualities… have been clearly seen\" (Romans 1:20). The Qur'an often points to signs (ayat) in nature, e.g. 3:190.\nStrengths: based on observation; complexity and fine-tuning are striking; many find it intuitively convincing.\nWeaknesses: David Hume argued we cannot compare the universe to a machine because we have no experience of universes being made; the design might come from many gods or a poor designer. Charles Darwin's theory of evolution by natural selection explains apparent design without a designer. Evil, suffering and natural disasters suggest a flawed design. The universe could have arisen by chance (and, if there are many universes, one would be suitable for life).\n\nTHE FIRST CAUSE ARGUMENT\nThomas Aquinas (Second and Third Ways): everything that exists has a cause; a chain of causes cannot go back infinitely; so there must be a first cause which was not itself caused – God. Kalam argument (Islamic origin, e.g. al-Ghazali): everything that begins to exist has a cause; the universe began to exist; so it has a cause. The Big Bang is seen by some theists as supporting a beginning. Genesis 1:1 \"In the beginning God created…\"\nStrengths: matches the common-sense idea that things have causes; fits with the Big Bang suggesting a beginning.\nWeaknesses: if everything needs a cause, what caused God? (A theist reply: God is eternal and necessary, so needs no cause.) Why can't the universe be infinite, or itself uncaused? Bertrand Russell argued that the universe is 'just there, and that's all'. Even if there is a first cause, it need not be the loving God of religion. The Big Bang itself could be the first cause.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nAtheists and humanists usually find these arguments unconvincing and prefer scientific explanations; Richard Dawkins argues that any designer would need to be even more complex than the universe and so needs explaining too."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-2","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.2","title":"Miracles, evil and suffering, and scientific arguments against God","specFocus":"The argument from miracles, including its strengths and weaknesses, and one example of a miracle; evil and suffering as an argument against the existence of God; arguments based on science against the existence of God. Contrasting beliefs on miracles (Christianity v non-religious beliefs) are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nMiracle: a seemingly impossible event, usually good, that cannot be explained by the laws of nature and is believed to be caused by God. Moral evil: suffering caused by humans acting wrongly (e.g. murder, war, terrorism). Natural evil: suffering caused by nature (e.g. earthquakes, disease, floods). Theodicy: an attempt to explain why God allows evil. Free will: the ability to make our own choices.\n\nTHE ARGUMENT FROM MIRACLES\nIf a miracle occurs, it must have a supernatural cause, which is God; therefore God exists. Examples: in the Bible, Jesus healing the sick, turning water into wine (John 2), raising Lazarus and his own resurrection. Example of a modern miracle: Lourdes in France, where Bernadette Soubirous reported visions of Mary in 1858; the Catholic Church's Lourdes Medical Bureau has officially recognised around 70 healings (e.g. Sister Bernadette Moriau, recognised in 2018 as the 70th). In Islam, the Qur'an itself is regarded as the greatest miracle; the Night Journey (Isra and Mi'raj) is also described. Sikhs recount stories of Guru Nanak, but Sikhism discourages seeking miracles.\nStrengths: personal experience can be powerful; there are many reports across religions; some events are hard to explain; miracles show God's love and involvement.\nWeaknesses: David Hume argued it is always more likely that a witness is mistaken or lying than that a law of nature was broken; many miracle stories come from religious people who want to believe; there may be scientific or psychological explanations (e.g. spontaneous remission, the placebo effect); coincidence; why would God perform some miracles but not prevent the Holocaust? Different religions report miracles that support conflicting beliefs.\n\nEVIL AND SUFFERING\nThe 'inconsistent triad' (associated with Epicurus and J.L. Mackie): if God is omnipotent and omnibenevolent, God would be able and willing to remove evil; but evil exists; so such a God cannot exist.\nReligious responses: free will – God gave humans freedom, and moral evil is the result of its misuse (Genesis 3, the Fall). John Hick's 'soul-making' theodicy – suffering helps people grow in virtue, such as compassion and courage. The Book of Job – human beings cannot understand God's purposes (\"Where were you when I laid the earth's foundation?\", Job 38:4). Christians believe God shares in suffering through Jesus on the cross, and they respond with prayer and practical help. Islam: suffering is a test from Allah (\"We will surely test you with something of fear and hunger…\", Qur'an 2:155). Buddhism: dukkha (suffering) is part of existence, caused by craving (tanha); it is not a problem for belief in God because Buddhists do not believe in a creator God. Hinduism: suffering results from karma.\n\nSCIENCE AGAINST GOD\nBig Bang theory and evolution explain the universe and life without a creator; medicine explains many 'miracles'; Ockham's razor says choose the simplest explanation. Responses: science explains 'how', religion explains 'why'; many scientists are religious; science cannot explain what caused the Big Bang.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nAtheists see the problem of evil as the strongest argument against God; humanists reject miracles as lacking evidence and emphasise scientific explanations."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-3","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.3","title":"Special revelation, visions and enlightenment","specFocus":"Special revelation as a source of knowledge about the divine (God, gods or ultimate reality), including visions and one example of a vision; enlightenment as a source of knowledge about the divine. Contrasting beliefs on visions (Christianity v non-religious beliefs) are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nRevelation: God (or the divine) making itself known to humans. Special revelation: God revealed directly to a particular person or group, e.g. through a vision, dream, prophecy or miracle. Vision: seeing something of spiritual significance, either with the eyes (a corporeal vision) or in the mind (an intellectual/imaginative vision), such as a saint, angel or God. Enlightenment: in Buddhism, gaining true knowledge of the nature of reality, ending suffering and rebirth (nibbana); in Hinduism and Sikhism, related ideas of moksha and mukti. The divine: God, gods or ultimate reality.\n\nEXAMPLES OF VISIONS\nChristianity: Saul (Paul) on the road to Damascus saw a light from heaven and heard Jesus ask, \"Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?\" (Acts 9:1–19); he was blinded, then baptised, and became a leading apostle. Other examples: the Transfiguration (Mark 9); Bernadette Soubirous at Lourdes (1858); the children at Fatima (1917); Julian of Norwich's 'Revelations of Divine Love'. Joan of Arc reported visions of saints that guided her.\nOld Testament: Isaiah's vision of God in the Temple (Isaiah 6); Moses and the burning bush (Exodus 3).\nIslam: the Prophet Muhammad's revelation from the angel Jibril (Gabriel) on Mount Hira in 610 CE – \"Read! In the name of your Lord who created\" (Qur'an 96:1).\nSikhism: Guru Nanak's experience in the river Bein, where he disappeared for three days and returned saying \"There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim\".\n\nENLIGHTENMENT\nSiddhattha Gotama meditated under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya and became the Buddha ('awakened one'), understanding the Four Noble Truths. Enlightenment comes through meditation and following the Eightfold Path, rather than from a God. For Buddhists, the 'divine' may be understood as ultimate reality rather than a personal God.\n\nWHY VISIONS MATTER\nThey can give certainty, change lives (e.g. Paul), offer guidance and strengthen the faith of a community. Places linked to visions such as Lourdes become pilgrimage sites.\n\nPROBLEMS AND ALTERNATIVE EXPLANATIONS\nThey are private and cannot be tested; they might be caused by hallucinations, drugs, alcohol, illness such as epilepsy or psychosis, lack of sleep or fasting; wishful thinking; some people may lie for attention or money; different religions report visions supporting contradictory beliefs. The Catholic Church investigates claimed visions carefully, and in 2024 issued new norms stressing caution; it approves very few.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nAtheists and humanists argue visions are best explained by psychology or neuroscience; Sigmund Freud saw religious experience as wish-fulfilment. They note that experiences seem to match the person's existing culture and religion – Catholics see Mary, Hindus see Krishna – suggesting they come from the mind. Some agnostics accept that people have genuine experiences, but say we cannot know what causes them."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper3-c-4","paper":"Theme C","module":"C.4","title":"General revelation, ideas about the divine and the value of revelation","specFocus":"General revelation: nature and scripture as a way of understanding the divine; different ideas about the divine from these sources (omnipotent and omniscient, personal and impersonal, immanent and transcendent); the value of general and special revelation and enlightenment as sources of knowledge about the divine, including the problems of different ideas about the divine and alternative explanations (the possibility that people were lying or mistaken). Contrasting beliefs on nature as general revelation (Christianity v non-religious beliefs) are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nGeneral revelation: God made known through ordinary, everyday ways available to everyone – especially nature, scripture, conscience and reason. Omnipotent: all-powerful. Omniscient: all-knowing. Omnibenevolent: all-loving. Personal: God has human-like qualities and can have a relationship with people (e.g. God as Father). Impersonal: the divine is a force or reality without human characteristics. Immanent: God is present in and involved with the world. Transcendent: God is beyond and outside the world, space and time.\n\nNATURE AS GENERAL REVELATION\nChristians believe the order and beauty of nature show God's power and creativity: \"The heavens declare the glory of God; the skies proclaim the work of his hands\" (Psalm 19:1); \"God's invisible qualities… have been clearly seen, being understood from what has been made\" (Romans 1:20). Feelings of awe and wonder (e.g. at a mountain view or the birth of a baby) can lead people to God; this is linked to the Design argument. Muslims see nature as full of signs (ayat) of Allah (Qur'an 3:190). Hindus see Brahman present in all of nature. Non-religious view: nature is beautiful and awe-inspiring but can be explained through science (evolution, physics); nature also contains cruelty and disasters, which may suggest no loving designer.\n\nSCRIPTURE AS GENERAL REVELATION\nChristians believe the Bible is inspired by God (\"All Scripture is God-breathed\", 2 Timothy 3:16); some read it literally, others as human writers inspired by God. Muslims believe the Qur'an is the direct word of Allah. Jews study the Torah. Sikhs treat the Guru Granth Sahib as the living Guru. Atheists see scripture as written by humans, reflecting their times.\n\nIDEAS ABOUT THE DIVINE\nChristianity: God is omnipotent (\"with God all things are possible\", Matthew 19:26), omniscient and omnibenevolent; both transcendent (creator beyond the universe) and immanent (in Jesus, the incarnation, and the Holy Spirit); personal (Jesus teaches people to pray 'Our Father'). Islam: Allah is transcendent (\"There is nothing like unto Him\", Qur'an 42:11) yet immanent (\"We are closer to him than his jugular vein\", 50:16); described by the 99 names. Hinduism: Brahman is ultimate reality, impersonal (nirguna) but also worshipped in personal forms such as Vishnu and Krishna (saguna). Buddhism: no creator God; ultimate reality is understood through enlightenment.\nProblems: some qualities seem to conflict – e.g. a personal, immanent God and a transcendent God; omnipotence and omnibenevolence and the existence of evil.\n\nTHE VALUE OF REVELATION\nFor: revelation offers knowledge that reason alone cannot reach; experiences transform lives; nature and scripture are open to everyone. Against: different religions describe the divine in different ways, so they cannot all be right; experiences may be explained by psychology or science; people may be mistaken, deceived or lying; scripture was written by humans and can be interpreted in many ways. Responses: some argue that different religions are seeing different aspects of the same ultimate reality, like the parable of the blind men and the elephant (used by Jains, Hindus and Buddhists).\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists rely on reason, evidence and science rather than revelation; atheists argue that if God wanted to be known, revelation would be clearer and less contradictory."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-1","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.1","title":"Peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, violent protest and terrorism","specFocus":"The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation; violence, including violent protest; terrorism. Contrasting beliefs on violence (Christianity + one or more other religious traditions) are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nPeace: the absence of conflict, and more positively a state of harmony and wellbeing (Hebrew shalom, Arabic salaam). Justice: bringing about what is right and fair; making up for wrongs. Forgiveness: showing mercy and pardoning someone for what they have done wrong. Reconciliation: restoring friendly relationships after conflict. Violence: using actions that threaten or harm others. Protest: expressing disapproval, often publicly. Terrorism: the unlawful use of violence, usually against civilians, to further a political, religious or ideological cause.\n\nCHRISTIANITY\nJesus is called the 'Prince of Peace' (Isaiah 9:6); \"Blessed are the peacemakers\" (Matthew 5:9). Jesus taught forgiveness \"seventy-seven times\" (Matthew 18:22) and forgave those who crucified him (Luke 23:34). \"Do not repay anyone evil for evil\" (Romans 12:17); \"turn the other cheek\" (Matthew 5:39). Justice: \"Let justice roll on like a river\" (Amos 5:24). Most Christians support peaceful protest (e.g. Martin Luther King Jr's non-violent civil rights campaign). Violence is generally rejected, though Jesus overturned the money-changers' tables in the Temple (John 2) – some argue this shows righteous anger. Christians condemn terrorism because it kills innocent people, breaking \"Do not kill\". Coventry Cathedral, bombed in 1940, became a centre of reconciliation (the Community of the Cross of Nails).\n\nISLAM\n'Islam' comes from the same root as salaam (peace); Muslims greet each other with \"As-salamu alaykum\". Allah is al-Adl (the Just) and al-Ghaffar (the Forgiving). \"If they incline to peace, then incline to it also\" (Qur'an 8:61). Violence is only permitted in self-defence and within strict rules; terrorism is condemned because it kills innocents – \"whoever kills a soul… it is as if he had slain mankind entirely\" (5:32) – and \"There is no compulsion in religion\" (2:256). UK Muslim leaders, for example the Muslim Council of Britain, have repeatedly condemned terrorist attacks such as the Manchester Arena bombing (2017).\n\nOTHER RELIGIONS\nBuddhism: the first Precept (ahimsa – non-harming) and karuna (compassion); \"Hatred is never appeased by hatred… by non-hatred alone is hatred appeased\" (Dhammapada 5). The Dalai Lama campaigns non-violently for Tibet. Sikhism: violence is a last resort to defend the oppressed; protest against injustice. Judaism: seeking peace (Psalm 34:14 \"seek peace and pursue it\"); tikkun olam (repairing the world). Hinduism: Gandhi's satyagraha ('truth force'), non-violent resistance.\n\nPROTEST IN THE UK\nThe right to peaceful protest is protected by the Human Rights Act 1998 (Article 11), though public order laws restrict some protests. Violent protest (e.g. riots) is illegal and condemned by most religious people because it harms innocent people and property.\n\nTERRORISM\nExamples: 7/7 London bombings (2005); Manchester Arena (2017). Some terrorists claim religious motives, but religious leaders say they misuse religion. Responses: interfaith work, condemnation, support for victims.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists support peace and justice based on reason and human rights; they accept peaceful protest and condemn terrorism and violence, though some accept force as a last resort in self-defence."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-2","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.2","title":"Reasons for war, just war theory, holy war and pacifism","specFocus":"Reasons for war, including greed, self-defence and retaliation; the just war theory, including the criteria for a just war; holy war; pacifism. Contrasting beliefs on pacifism are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nWar: armed conflict between nations or groups. Greed: selfish desire for more (land, resources, power). Self-defence: acting to protect oneself or others. Retaliation: deliberately harming someone in response to their harming you. Just war: a war that meets internationally accepted criteria for fairness. Holy war: fighting for a religious cause or God, often controlled by a religious leader. Pacifism: the belief that people should not take part in war or any violence.\n\nREASONS FOR WAR\nGreed – e.g. Iraq's invasion of Kuwait in 1990 was partly about oil. Self-defence – e.g. Ukraine resisting the Russian invasion (2022–). Retaliation – e.g. the US-led invasion of Afghanistan after the 9/11 attacks (2001). Religious teaching generally condemns greed (\"the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil\", 1 Timothy 6:10) and revenge, but many accept self-defence.\n\nJUST WAR THEORY\nDeveloped by St Augustine (4th–5th century) and Thomas Aquinas (13th century). Criteria include: (1) just cause – e.g. self-defence or protecting the innocent; (2) correct/legitimate authority – declared by a lawful government; (3) right intention – to promote good and end evil, not for revenge or greed; (4) last resort – all peaceful methods tried; (5) reasonable chance of success; (6) proportionality – the harm caused must not be greater than the harm prevented, and only necessary force used, with civilians not targeted. The Catholic Church sets out similar conditions (Catechism 2309). The Islamic tradition also has rules: fighting only in defence, not harming women, children, the elderly or crops, and making peace if the enemy offers it (\"Fight in the way of Allah those who fight you but do not transgress\", Qur'an 2:190).\n\nHOLY WAR\nChristianity: the Crusades (1095–1291) were called by popes to recapture Jerusalem. Most Christians today reject holy war. Islam: lesser jihad is a struggle in defence of Islam under strict conditions; greater jihad is the personal struggle to live a good Muslim life, which the Prophet called the greater struggle. Terrorist groups who claim to fight jihad are condemned by the vast majority of Muslims. Judaism: milchemet mitzvah (obligatory war) in the Hebrew Bible, e.g. defending Israel. Sikhism: dharam yudh (war in defence of righteousness) with criteria similar to just war, e.g. last resort, no looting.\n\nPACIFISM\nChristian pacifists, such as the Quakers (Religious Society of Friends), refuse to fight: \"all war is contrary to the spirit of Christ\" (Quaker Peace Testimony, 1660). They cite \"Blessed are the peacemakers\" (Matthew 5:9), \"love your enemies\" (Matthew 5:44) and Jesus telling Peter \"all who draw the sword will die by the sword\" (Matthew 26:52). In both World Wars, conscientious objectors refused to fight; some served as medics. Buddhists are often pacifist because of the first Precept; the Dalai Lama promotes non-violence. Jains practise strict ahimsa. Many Hindus follow Gandhi's non-violence, though the Bhagavad Gita describes Arjuna's duty (dharma) as a warrior to fight.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nMany humanists accept just war ideas as a last resort to protect human rights, while some are pacifists; they reject holy war because they see no divine authority for violence."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-3","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.3","title":"Religion as a cause of conflict; nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction","specFocus":"Religion and belief as a cause of war and violence in the contemporary world; nuclear weapons, including nuclear deterrence; the use of weapons of mass destruction. Contrasting beliefs on weapons of mass destruction are required.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nWeapons of mass destruction (WMD): weapons that can kill large numbers of people and cause great damage – nuclear, chemical and biological weapons. Nuclear weapons: explosive devices using nuclear reactions (e.g. the bombs dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, killing over 100,000 people, many from radiation later). Chemical weapons: toxic chemicals such as sarin (used in Syria, e.g. Ghouta 2013). Biological weapons: using bacteria or viruses (e.g. anthrax). Nuclear deterrence: having nuclear weapons to discourage an enemy from attacking. Disarmament: getting rid of weapons.\n\nRELIGION AS A CAUSE OF CONFLICT\nSome argue religion causes conflict because groups believe they alone have the truth, or use religion to justify violence – e.g. Islamist terrorist groups such as ISIS; tensions involving religious identity in Northern Ireland (the Troubles, 1960s–1998, though mainly about national identity and civil rights); violence between Hindus and Muslims in parts of India; the Israel–Palestine conflict, which has religious, national and territorial dimensions. Others argue religion is often blamed for conflicts that are really about land, power, politics or ethnicity, and that religion is more often a force for peace. Richard Dawkins argues religion fuels division; most religious leaders say violent extremists misuse religion.\n\nNUCLEAR WEAPONS AND DETERRENCE\nThe UK has Trident nuclear missiles on submarines. The Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons (1968) aims to stop their spread; the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons (2017, in force 2021) bans them for countries that sign (the UK has not). Arguments for deterrence: it has helped prevent large-scale war between nuclear powers since 1945 (\"mutually assured destruction\"); it protects a country against nuclear blackmail. Arguments against: it is immoral to threaten to kill millions of civilians; there is a risk of accidents or of weapons reaching terrorists; it is hugely expensive; it encourages other countries to build them.\n\nRELIGIOUS VIEWS ON WMD\nChristianity: the Catholic Church teaches \"every act of war directed to the indiscriminate destruction of entire cities… is a crime against God and man\" (Second Vatican Council, Gaudium et Spes 80). Pope Francis (2019, at Hiroshima and Nagasaki) declared that even the possession of nuclear weapons is immoral. The Church of England has generally opposed their use while debating deterrence; some Christians accept deterrence as a lesser evil. WMD fail the just war criteria of proportionality and protecting civilians. Christian CND campaigns for disarmament.\nIslam: WMD kill indiscriminately, and the Prophet forbade killing women, children and non-combatants; the Qur'an condemns those who \"spread corruption on earth\". Some Muslims argue deterrence may be necessary for self-defence.\nBuddhism: WMD break the first Precept and cause vast suffering. Sikhism and Hinduism: dharam yudh/just war rules forbid harming civilians; Judaism: the principle of pikuach nefesh and the prophetic vision of beating \"swords into ploughshares\" (Isaiah 2:4).\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists generally oppose WMD as a threat to human life and future generations; some accept deterrence as a practical necessity while working for multilateral disarmament."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-themes-paper4-d-4","paper":"Theme D","module":"D.4","title":"Religion and peace-making; responses to the victims of war","specFocus":"Religion and peace-making in the contemporary world, including the work of individuals influenced by religious teaching; religious responses to the victims of war, including the work of one present-day religious organisation.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"KEY TERMS\nPeace-making: working actively to bring about peace and reconciliation. Peacekeeping: maintaining peace, e.g. UN forces. Victims of war: civilians and soldiers killed or injured, refugees and displaced people, families who lose loved ones. Refugee: a person forced to flee their country because of war or persecution. Humanitarian aid: practical help such as food, shelter and medicine.\n\nINDIVIDUALS INFLUENCED BY RELIGIOUS TEACHING\nArchbishop Desmond Tutu (Anglican, South Africa): opposed apartheid non-violently and chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission (1996–98), which allowed victims and perpetrators to speak and offered amnesty in return for full truth. Nobel Peace Prize 1984.\nMartin Luther King Jr (Baptist minister, USA): non-violent civil rights campaigner inspired by the Sermon on the Mount.\nFr Alec Reid, a Catholic priest, played an important part in the Northern Ireland peace process leading to the Good Friday Agreement (1998).\nLeymah Gbowee (Christian, Liberia): organised Christian and Muslim women to pray and protest for peace, helping end the Liberian civil war in 2003; Nobel Peace Prize 2011.\nThich Nhat Hanh (Buddhist monk, Vietnam): campaigned for peace during the Vietnam War; taught 'engaged Buddhism'.\nMalala Yousafzai (Muslim): campaigner for girls' education and peace.\nGandhi (Hindu): satyagraha, non-violent resistance.\n\nORGANISATIONS FOR PEACE\nPax Christi (Catholic international peace movement); the Fellowship of Reconciliation; Coventry Cathedral's Community of the Cross of Nails; the Corrymeela Community in Northern Ireland (Christian, bringing Catholics and Protestants together); Interfaith groups such as the Inter Faith Network.\n\nRESPONSES TO VICTIMS OF WAR\nTeaching: the Parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25–37) teaches love for anyone in need, including enemies; \"whatever you did for one of the least of these… you did for me\" (Matthew 25:40) – the parable of the sheep and goats. Islam: zakat and sadaqah; \"feed the hungry, visit the sick, free the captive\" (hadith, Bukhari). Sikhism: sewa (selfless service) and langar; Khalsa Aid gives emergency relief in war zones, e.g. Syria and Ukraine. Judaism: tzedakah (charity/justice) and gemilut chasadim (acts of loving-kindness); World Jewish Relief helps refugees.\nExample of a present-day religious organisation: Christian Aid – helps refugees and people affected by conflict with food, water, shelter and long-term support, works with local partners and campaigns for peace and justice; Caritas/CAFOD (Catholic) give emergency aid in conflict zones; Islamic Relief provides food, medical care and shelter, e.g. in Gaza, Yemen and Syria; Tearfund (evangelical Christian) works with churches in conflict areas.\nOthers: the Royal British Legion (secular) helps veterans; Red Cross/Red Crescent help all victims impartially.\n\nNON-RELIGIOUS VIEWS\nHumanists support peace-making and humanitarian aid based on compassion and human rights; Humanists UK campaigns for refugee rights."}],"rubrics":{"Theme A":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1): either a multiple-choice question with four options (one correct letter) or a short 'Give one…' question. Only the first answer given is credited.","12":"12 marks (AO2) – 'Evaluate this statement'. Levels of response: L4 (10–12) well-argued, reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s), religion applied to the issue; L3 (7–9) reasoned consideration of different views with clear reference to religion; L2 (4–6) reasoned consideration of one view, or recognition of different views each supported by reasons (maximum Level 2 if no reference to religion); L1 (1–3) point of view with reason(s). Plus up to 3 SPaG marks (high 3 / intermediate 2 / threshold 1) for spelling, punctuation, grammar and range of specialist terms.","4":"4 marks (AO1:3) – 'Explain two similar/different beliefs in contemporary British society about…' referring to the main religious tradition of Great Britain (Christianity) and one or more other religious traditions. For each belief: simple explanation of a relevant, accurate similarity/difference = 1 mark; detailed explanation = 2 marks. Required contrasting-belief issues for Theme A: contraception; sexual relationships before marriage; homosexual relationships.","6":"6 marks (AO1) – 'Explain two religious beliefs about… Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching.' Each belief: simple explanation = 1 mark, detailed = 2 marks (×2). Source of authority: 2 marks for naming and applying a relevant source; 1 mark for naming it only, or applying it without naming it."},"Theme B":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1): multiple-choice (one correct letter from A–D) or 'Give one…'. Only the first answer is credited.","12":"12 marks (AO2) – evaluate the statement: arguments for, arguments for a different view, religious arguments (maximum Level 2 without them), non-religious arguments optional, justified conclusion. L4 10–12, L3 7–9, L2 4–6, L1 1–3. Plus up to 3 SPaG marks.","4":"4 marks (AO1:3) – two similar/different beliefs in contemporary British society, referring to Christianity and one or more other religious traditions; each belief 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Required contrasting-belief issues for Theme B: abortion; euthanasia; animal experimentation.","6":"6 marks (AO1) – two religious beliefs (each 1 simple / 2 detailed) plus up to 2 marks for naming and applying sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching."},"Theme C":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1): multiple-choice (one correct letter from A–D) or 'Give one…'. Only the first answer is credited.","12":"12 marks (AO2) – evaluate the statement: arguments for, arguments for a different view, religious arguments (maximum Level 2 without them), non-religious arguments optional, justified conclusion. L4 10–12, L3 7–9, L2 4–6, L1 1–3. Plus up to 3 SPaG marks.","4":"4 marks (AO1:3) – two similar/different beliefs in contemporary British society. For Theme C the reference is to the main religious tradition of Great Britain (Christianity) and NON-RELIGIOUS beliefs such as atheism and humanism. Each belief 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Required issues: visions; miracles; nature as general revelation.","6":"6 marks (AO1) – two religious beliefs (each 1 simple / 2 detailed) plus up to 2 marks for naming and applying sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching."},"Theme D":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1): multiple-choice (one correct letter from A–D) or 'Give one…'. Only the first answer is credited.","12":"12 marks (AO2) – evaluate the statement: arguments for, arguments for a different view, religious arguments (maximum Level 2 without them), non-religious arguments optional, justified conclusion. L4 10–12, L3 7–9, L2 4–6, L1 1–3. Plus up to 3 SPaG marks.","4":"4 marks (AO1:3) – two similar/different beliefs in contemporary British society, referring to Christianity and one or more other religious traditions; each belief 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Required contrasting-belief issues for Theme D: violence; weapons of mass destruction; pacifism.","6":"6 marks (AO1) – two religious beliefs (each 1 simple / 2 detailed) plus up to 2 marks for naming and applying sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching."},"Theme E":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1): multiple-choice (one correct letter from A–D) or 'Give one…'. Only the first answer is credited.","12":"12 marks (AO2) – evaluate the statement: arguments for, arguments for a different view, religious arguments (maximum Level 2 without them), non-religious arguments optional, justified conclusion. L4 10–12, L3 7–9, L2 4–6, L1 1–3. Plus up to 3 SPaG marks.","4":"4 marks (AO1:3) – two similar/different beliefs in contemporary British society, referring to Christianity and one or more other religious traditions; each belief 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Required contrasting-belief issues for Theme E: corporal punishment; the death penalty; forgiveness.","6":"6 marks (AO1) – two religious beliefs (each 1 simple / 2 detailed) plus up to 2 marks for naming and applying sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching."},"Theme F":{"1":"1-mark questions (AO1): multiple-choice (one correct letter from A–D) or 'Give one…'. Only the first answer is credited.","12":"12 marks (AO2) – evaluate the statement: arguments for, arguments for a different view, religious arguments (maximum Level 2 without them), non-religious arguments optional, justified conclusion. L4 10–12, L3 7–9, L2 4–6, L1 1–3. Plus up to 3 SPaG marks.","4":"4 marks (AO1:3) – two similar/different beliefs in contemporary British society, referring to Christianity and one or more other religious traditions; each belief 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Required contrasting-belief issues for Theme F: status of women in religion; the uses of wealth; freedom of religious expression.","6":"6 marks (AO1) – two religious beliefs (each 1 simple / 2 detailed) plus up to 2 marks for naming and applying sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching."}},"moduleCounts":{"paper1":4,"paper2":4,"paper3":4,"paper4":4},"meta":{"modulesTotal":16,"paper1QuestionCount":24,"paper2QuestionCount":24,"paper3QuestionCount":24,"paper4QuestionCount":24,"paper5QuestionCount":0,"paper6QuestionCount":0,"provenance":"Original practice questions and revision notes written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification (Component 2, religious, philosophical and ethical themes A–F) and the question format and tariffs AQA publishes for exams from June 2026 (1, 1, 4, 6 and 12 marks per theme; 3 SPaG marks on the 12-mark question). Not past-paper material and not copied from AQA papers, mark schemes, textbooks or revision guides. Model answers are indicative exemplars, not official mark schemes. Short scripture quotations are cited in each question's evidence list.","qualification":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062), Component 2: Thematic studies (Paper 2A)","questionTotal":96,"title":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A – Thematic Studies (Themes A–F)"}});
